\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  cenbank may  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  to deter attack on peso -economists\b0\par\par\uc2 338 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2016\par 01:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, May 17 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso is under a speculative attack and the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could soon \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, an influential group of Mexican economists said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Hedge funds are testing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  through an attack on the peso, according to economists at the Mexican Institute for Finance Executives (IMEF), a trade group of local chief financial officers.\par \par \uc2 "The way to fight an exchange rate problem is with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, not monetary policy, as in this case, with the sale of foreign reserves," \uc2 Gabriel Casillas\uc2 , the IMEF's vice president of economic studies, said at a conference in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 Casillas, who is also chief economist at Mexican bank Banorte, said he believed the peso was under a "speculative attack."\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso has shed more than 6 percent this month, making it the second worst performer in May among the world's 36 most-traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , behind the South African rand.\par \par \uc2 In February, \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time since 2009 and unexpectedly raised its benchmark interest rate by 50 basis points to 3.75 percent.\par \par \uc2 At the time, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it acted after the peso experience losses that were not in line with the fundamentals of the Mexican economy, blaming electronic trading programs for attacking the peso.\par \par \uc2 "We think that at this time, given the current conditions, (\b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission) could turn to an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to calm markets a bit," IMEF president Joaquin Gandara said.\par \par \uc2 Other economists think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could use a combination of an interest rate hike and additional \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the peso.\par \par \uc2 Deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Javier Guzman said on Tuesday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may need to raise interest rates if global events hit the peso and inflation expectations. (Reporting by Luis Rojas; Writing by Michael O'Boyle; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-20T23:00:02.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160517ec5h02b3q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
