\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican Stocks, Peso Lose Ground on Falling Oil Prices\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amy Guthrie   \par \uc2 228 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2016\par 22:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 MEXICO CITY--Mexican stocks slipped while the peso lost ground against the U.S. dollar Tuesday as plunging oil prices chipped away at recent gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The IPC index of Mexico's most-traded shares lost 1% to 44504 points, putting an end to its eight-session winning streak.\par \par \uc2 And the peso finished around 17.9220 pesos to the U.S. dollar, compared with 17.7405 on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recovered significant ground since weakening to more than 19 pesos to the dollar in early February, helped by direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars on Feb. 17 unannounced in its first such foray into the market since the 2009 crisis, and vowed to sell again if the peso weakens substantially.\par \par \uc2 But Mexico is sensitive to falling oil prices because oil revenue from state-run oil company \uc2 Pemex\uc2  helps pad the federal budget. \uc2 Pemex\uc2  is also behind on paying its suppliers, which creates a ripple effect in the broader economy.\par \par \uc2 Among blue chip shares Tuesday, wireless carrier \uc2 America Movil\uc2  advanced 0.6% to 13.36 pesos and cement maker \uc2 Cemex\uc2  lost 3.5% to 10.61 pesos.\par \par \uc2 Write to Amy Guthrie at amy.guthrie@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 08, 2016 16:55 ET (21:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160308ec38004em\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picsca