\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-\uc2 Mexico  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  discussed possible interest rate rise in early Feb\b0\par\par\uc2 438 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 2016\par 17:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds detail, background)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Feb 18 (Reuters) - \uc2 Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymakers were unanimous in their Feb 4 decision to leave interest rates on hold, according to the minutes of the meeting reported on Thursday, but most added it was important to signal they could move at any time, as they subsequently did on Wednesday this week with a surprise rate hike.\par \par \uc2 At their early February meeting, policymakers voted 5 to 0 to keep their benchmark interest rate at 3.25 percent but a majority said monetary policy was in a complicated situation.\par \par \uc2 The minutes of the Feb. 4 meeting were published on Thursday, a day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised markets by delivering a 50 basis point interest rate rise to shore up Mexico's peso which had fallen by about 30 percent against the U.S. dollar in the past year. Mexico's peso ended up nearly 3.0 percent on Wednesday after the rate hike.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday also suspended daily auctions to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favor of extraordinary \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  during moments of high volatility.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso hit a fresh record low last week on tumbling crude oil prices. Mexico is the world's tenth largest oil producer and oil revenues account for about 10 percent of its exports and about one third of government spending, according to the U.S. Energy Information Agency. Crude oil prices have fallen more than 70 percent in the past two years.\par \par \uc2 In the Feb 4 decision, some members said they would need to hike interest rates if the recent volatility in global markets worsened and said they should move on rates whether pressures came from the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's policy changes or any other source.\par \par \uc2 Laying the groundwork for Thursday's surprise interest rate rise in the minutes of the Feb 4 meeting, the majority thought it was important to send the message that the bank could act with "flexibility" to adjust monetary policy at the right pace.\par \par \uc2 The majority also highlighted worsening risks to growth and inflation in the short term.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's annual inflation rose faster than expected in January from a record low, partly reflecting the slump in the peso.\par \par \uc2 Inflation in the 12 months through January rose to 2.61 percent, up from a 2.13 percent rate in December. Prior to Wednesday's rate rise, \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had raised interest rates in December. (Reporting by Michael O'Boyle and Alexandra Alper; Editing by Simon Gardner and Clive McKeef)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-2-21T16:18:32.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160218ec2i01l95\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
