\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 7-Mexico unleashes surprise rate hike to smoke out peso speculators\b0\par\par\uc2 750 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 2016\par 01:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Adds fund manager comment, updates prices)\par     By Miguel Gutierrez and Michael O'Boyle\par     MEXICO CITY, Feb 17 (Reuters) - Mexico on Wednesday launched\par an assault on speculators who have battered the local peso\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by unleashing a surprise interest rate hike, a new\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy and budget cuts.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's peso surged nearly 3 percent after the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  offered dollars directly to banks, and then\par unexpectedly raised its benchmark interest rate by\par 50 basis points to 3.75 percent.\par     It was the first time it has moved without advising the\par market the board was meeting since introducing a reference rate\par in 2008. \par     The Finance Ministry also announced cuts to the 2016 budget\par in a bid to shore up confidence in Mexico's economy following a\par collapse in oil prices that hobbled government revenue from\par crude sales.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Agustin Carstens\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the board\par decided to hike to help stem peso losses and keep inflation\par expectations from spiking higher and risking an upward spiral in\par consumer prices, which rose off a record low in January.\par     The peso had been the most-punished, highly traded \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par in the global market rout of early 2016, slumping to a record\par low near 19.50 per dollar. But Carstens said that the peso had\par been unfairly hammered and was not trading on its own\par fundamentals.\par     "This is not the beginning of a cycle of interest rate\par hikes," he said. "It was a way of showing our protest, our clear\par rejection of those levels" of the peso against the dollar.\par     Mexico's peso, one of the world's most liquid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , is\par often used as a money-making short when general sentiment turns\par sour against emerging market assets.\par     Shares of companies with high dollar-debt exposure, such as\par cement company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cemex\f28 \uc2 \f2 , were sharply higher.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cemex\f28 \uc2 \f2  jumped 8.9 percent, while Banorte\par  rose 6.4 percent since higher interest rates boost\par banks' balance sheets.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to sell dollars marks a major policy shift to\par support the peso.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco de Mexico\f28 \uc2 \f2  had sold dollars in rules-based auctions\par since a deep slump in the peso in 2014. Wednesday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par was the first time the bank opted for direct dollar sales since\par the 2009 financial crisis. \par     Carstens said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was ending the daily auction\par regime, where it sold up to $400 million on days of big peso\par losses, since it had become anticipated by market players. He\par suggested the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  made its bold move to smoke out some\par specific betting strategies against the Mexican peso.\par     "There were many other strategies that were being used and\par that were present in the market that were destabilizing the\par exchange rate and taking it to a level that was not appropriate\par for the performance of the Mexican economy," he said. \par     Finance Minister Luis Videgaray said the government would\par make spending cuts to the 2016 budget to the tune of 132 billion\par pesos ($7.23 billion), or about 0.7 percent of gross domestic\par product. State oil company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pemex\f28 \uc2 \f2  would account for 100 billion\par pesos of those expenditure cuts, he said. \par     Fund managers applauded Mexico for its timing. Before the\par move, the peso was gaining due to a rally in the oil market that\par was already forcing speculators to unwind bets against Mexico. \par     "It's the perfect positive storm for Mexico and that helps\par the Mexican peso," said Ruggero de'Rossi, a senior portfolio\par manager for emerging market debt at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federated Investors\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   at one point gained nearly 5\par percent, breaking past the 18 level to 17.962 per dollar, but it\par pared back gains for the day to 2.8 percent at 18.36 per dollar,\par still its biggest one-day gain since September 2011. \par     Not all market analysts welcomed the move.\par     "They are putting all the meat on the grill and they could\par end up with no tools when things get even worse," said Marco\par Oviedo, an economist at Barclays in Mexico City.\par     Mexico's economy has slowed as uneven U.S. demand for its\par exports and tumbling oil prices have hit industrial production,\par but improving consumer spending has helped support growth. \par \par     \par  (Additional reporting by Elinor Comlay, Alexandra Alper and\par Adriana Barrera in Mexico City, Jennifer Ablan in New York and\par Tariro Mzezewa in London; Writing by Simon Gardner; Editing by W\par Simon and Matthew Lewis)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-2-21T00:38:49.000Z \par \p