\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico's Rate Hike: A Sign Emerging Markets Selloff Is Over? -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dimitra DeFotis   \par \uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2016\par 20:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 With emerging market stocks rallying this week along with oil prices, the timing of the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise rate hike Wednesday was impeccable, and is a sign the emerging market selloff is bottoming, \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  says.\par \par \uc2 It is higher oil prices helping boost developing world stocks today, though Perhaps Pope Francis instigated a bit of divine \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during his visit to \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  City over the weekend. The iShares MSCI Emerging Market exchange-traded fund ( EEM), which was up nearly 2% in recent trading. The iShares MSCI Mexico Capped exchange-traded fund ( \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EWW) was up 3.8%.   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cemex\f28 \uc2 \f2  ( CX) shares were up more than 10%. The iShares South Africa ETF ( EZA) was up 3% and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 iShares MSCI Brazil Capped ETF\f28 \uc2 \f2  ( EWZ) was up nearly 4%. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Banxico\f28 \uc2 \f2  raised its interbank borrowing rate by 50 basis points to 3.75% in a surprise move Wednesday. While the yield on local 10-year bonds was flat at near 6%, the peso rallied against the dollar, and the Mexican stock market is higher, having recovered from initial losses. BBH writes:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging markets, oil, and "risk" were already rallying this week and so \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banxico\f28 \uc2 \f2  appears to have picked the perfect timing. Indeed, Mexico's actions can be filed perfectly under the "Golden Rules" of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : 1) surprise the markets, 2) go big, and 3) go WITH the market, not against it. ... Mexico demonstrates its long track record of policymaking ingenuity and knowhow. Despite a reputation for being largely hands-off, policymakers there have served notice to the markets that there is always two-way risk present.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Brazilian real (BRL) and the South African rand (ZAR) are up nearly 2% each today, RUB up 3.7% in a spillover effect. BBH notes:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It looks like the moves in Mexico are having some positive spillover to the rest of emerging markets. With global equities rallying and commodity prices rebounding, it's tempting to say that the worst for emerging markets is over. We are not convinced, but we acknowledge that the near-term environment should allow this emerging market bounce to continue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Capital Economics\f28 \uc2 \f2  observes that the Mexican government also announced budget cuts with the rate hike Wednesday in a combined effort to strengthen the peso. However,\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Stabilising the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  [the Mexican peso] over the longer term will ultimately depend on whether oil prices bottom out and sentiment towards emerging markets improves. We suspect it will. But the policy tightening today will weigh on the real economy too. We've marked down our GDP growth forecast for this year from 3% to 2.5%." \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  More at Barron's Emerging Markets blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 17, 2016 14:25 ET (19:25 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160217ec2h003nr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
