\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Mexico peso sinks to record low, Colombia slumps\b0\par\par\uc2 492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2016\par 21:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par MEXICO CITY, Feb 9 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso fell to a\par record low on Tuesday and Colombia's peso sank as a sharp drop\par in oil prices and growing fears of a global slowdown pushed\par investors to safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the Japanese yen.\par     Mexico's peso  shed more than 0.7 percent,\par paring losses slightly after tumbling to a record low of 18.9350\par per dollar, while Colombia's peso shed more than 0.8\par percent as it trades near a record low.\par     Oil prices fell 6 percent. Both Colombia and Mexico are\par major crude producers, but Colombia is more dependent on oil\par exports than Mexico, which ships mostly factory-made goods.\par     Still, the drop in crude prices has dampened prospects for a\par windfall of foreign direct investment into Mexico in the first\par private oil fields that have gone up for auction under the\par country's landmark energy reform.\par     But analysts said the peso was also being hammered because\par it is one of the most liquid global emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par making it a sure bet when global sentiment turns sour, as well\par as a move by investors to hedge big positions in local\par bonds. \par     The global forces moving against the peso have kept local\par authorities from increasing their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  efforts to\par support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which are currently limited to selling up\par to $400 million a day in auctions when the peso sees big moves. \par     Mexican \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Agustin Carstens\f28 \uc2 \f2  said on\par Tuesday that the current levels of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are\par "adequate." Late last month, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  extended its current regime\par of auctions through the end of March. \par     Markets in Brazil were closed due to the Carnival holiday.\par     Mexico's IPC index slid about 0.6 percent as shares\par of billionaire \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carlos Slim\f28 \uc2 \f2 's \f28 \uc2 \f2 America Movil\f28 \uc2 \f2  shed 2.5 percent\par ahead of the release of its fourth quarter earnings statement\par later on Tuesday. \par     \par  Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 2000 GMT:\par     \par  Stock indexes                       daily %    YTD %\par                             Latest    change   change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 MSCI\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets       729.69     -0.6    -7.56\par                                               \par  MSCI LatAm                 1724.49    -0.45    -5.33\par                                               \par  Brazil Bovespa            40592.09    -0.56    -6.36\par                                               \par  Mexico IPC                42499.44    -0.64    -1.11\par                                               \par  Chile IPSA                 3652.15     0.02    -0.76\par                                               \par  Chile IGPA                17955.20    -0.04    -1.08\par                                               \par  Argentina MerVal          11400.34    -0.18    -2.35\par                                               \par  Colombia IGBC              8703.97     0.03     1.83\par                                               \par  Venezuela IBC             14372.64    -2.03    -1.48\par                                              