\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Move a Sign of Tolerance -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,285 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2015\par 22:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 16:49 ET - By slowing the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission is signaling that the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  is willing to tolerate moderate but additional MXN weakness, says \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . It also suggests any local monetary tightening will be less aggressive than interest-rate markets are pricing in. As of Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stop selling $200M every day, but continue to auction $200M on days when the peso weakens 1% vs \uc2 USD\uc2 , with a backup $200M more if the peso's slide then extends to 1.5%. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:50 ET - Global deals in the tech sector have reached new heights. Volume has already reached $650.9B so far this year, surpassing the last full-year record set in 2000, when it was $447B, according to Dealogic. The number of deals in the sector hit an annual record too, with 8,320 transactions this year-to-date, compared with 7,795 in 2014. \uc2 Dell\uc2 's $65.7B bid for \uc2 EMC\uc2  (expected to close in 2016) remained the largest tech deal on record. The bulk of these deals were inked in North America, with $429.5B so far this year, more than double the volume from this period last year. (Lillian.Rizzo@wsj.com; @Lilliannnn)\par \par \uc2 16:45 ET - Noble Midstream has postponed its plans to be the first MLP IPO since June, citing "unfavorable equity market conditions." Up until recently, MLPs were one of the most popular investments on Wall Street due to their tax benefits, steadily growing distributions to investors and perceived insulation from big oil-price fluctuations. But the sector has sold off considerably this year, with the \uc2 NYSE\uc2  Alerian MLP index off a third in 2015. However, so far this week the index is actually up 1.1%, though that gain lags the 2.9% gain in the S&P 500 during the same period. Noble Midstream had aimed to sell 12.5M shares at $19-$21 apiece, raising $250M at the midpoint. (corrie.driebusch@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:40 ET - Chesapeake CEO Doug Lawler signaled his faith in the company's turnaround earlier this week, buying 50K shares at $5.88 -- or $294K total. He now owns 701,400 shares, according to a \uc2 SEC\uc2  filing. Lawler was brought in to lead Chesapeake in 2013, after its previous CEO was ousted by activist investors. But the company's stock has fallen sharply amid the oil bust. It has dropped from nearly $20 at the start of the 2015 to Thursday's close of $5.40. (erin.ailworth@wsj.com; @ailworth)\par \par \uc2 16:30 ET - \uc2 Tyson Foods\uc2  (TSN) announces plans to close more plants and lay off more workers after announcing plans in August to shutter an Iowa beef-processing facility. Tyson says it will close in the second half of 2016 a pepperoni-making plant in Wisconsin and a Chicago facility producing meatballs, omelets and soups, citing the cost of renovating the aging facilities and the Chicago plant's distance from the source of raw materials. About 880 jobs are affected, though Tyson will encourage employees to apply for other openings in the company. Meatpackers this year have grappled with constrained overseas sales, due to slowing economic growth in some countries and curbs on poultry exports due to the U.S. bird flu outbreak, which has boosted supply on the U.S. market. (jacob.bunge@wsj.com; @jacobbunge)\par \par \uc2 16:06 ET - The major US stock indexes wanted to hug the flat line again today following Wednesday's bounce. But certainly not everything did as energy and health insurers showed noted weakness. But their declines weren't enough to push the averages far from Wednesday's finishing levels. The Dow industrials fell 4.41 points to 17732.75, the S&P 500 shed 2.33 to 2081.25 and the Nasdaq Composite eased 1.56 to 5073.64. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 15:50 ET - Oil producers have added 339K barrels/day of new crude-output capacity in the US this month, says DrillingInfo. That's 5% decline than October, reflecting continued spending cuts by US producers. DrillingInfo tracks drilling rigs and permits and says new production capacity is an indicator of output 5-6 months from now. (nicole.friedman@wsj.com; @NicoleFriedman)\par \par \uc2 15:40 ET - The Brazilian unit of Spanish bank Santander (SAN) will focus mainly on organic growth but it's open to possible acquisitions of local banks, Chairman Ana Botin tells reporters. Santander, which has been through other crises in Brazil, won't change its investments plans for the country because of the current economic downturn, Botin says. Brazil is the second most important market for Santander, only behind England, she says. Santander has recently studied the acquisition of \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's assets in Brazil, but they ended up being sold to Brazilian bank \uc2 Bradesco\uc2 . (luciana.magalaes@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:38 ET - There have now been 99 global defaults this year by S&P's count, but this week an oil-and-gas company isn't one of them. The bankruptcy filings of lab-test provider Millennium Health and Affirmative Insurance added to the list as 2015's total is the 2nd highest in 11 years, topped only by 2009's 222 by November. This year's jump has been driven by distress in the oil patch amid the price slump there. (stephanie.gleason@wsj.com; @stephgleason)\par \par \uc2 15:22 ET - The Navajo Nation sold almost $53M of BBB+ rated debt this week in the first bond sale from the largest Native American tribe, says KeyBanc, which led the transaction. The general obligation bonds will be used to refinance outstanding bank debt borrowed to build judicial and public safety buildings. "The Navajo Nation has entered a new era of leveraging its financial assets to fund infrastructure projects," Russell Begaye, president of Navajo Nation says. The Navajo Nation is a federally recognized tribe and sovereign nation with more than 285,000 members, with a reservation spanning parts of Arizona, New Mexico and Utah and headquarters in Window Rock, Ariz. "This landmark transaction confirms investors' confidence in the Navajo Nation," said \uc2 Geoff Urbina\uc2 , a KeyBanc managing director. (aaron.kurlioff@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:20 ET - \uc2 Airbus\uc2  (AIR.FR) feels no urgency to offer a new version of its A380 jetliner, the head of the company's jetliner unit said today in New York, insisting \uc2 Airbus\uc2  must wait until customer demand for a new-engine option of the plane materializes. \uc2 Fabrice Bregier\uc2  told a gathering of the Wings Club that the company will eventually build an A380neo (new engine option) but first \uc2 Airbus\uc2  "will need a business case" and sufficient customers to justify development costs. Asked to comment on remarks by \uc2 Emirates Airline\uc2  that it could boost its orders if \uc2 Airbus\uc2  developed such a plane and that it might do so by 2022, Bregier waved them off, saying the date "for me is too early." (ted.mann@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:13 ET - The issue of guns, and limits on their use, is under fresh scrutiny in the wake of the terrorist attacks in Paris. Rep. Peter King (R., NY) and \uc2 Sen. Dianne Feinstein\uc2  (D., Calif.) this week reiterated their support for a bill, introduced in February, which would grant the US attorney general the authority to ban gun sales to anyone suspected of terrorism-related activities. The \uc2 NRA\uc2  and many lawmakers oppose the legislation, partly on grounds it sweeps too broadly. (ashby.jones@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 