\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Mexico extends dollar auctions after peso slumps to record low\b0\par\par\uc2 231 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2015\par 16:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds market reaction, background)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Sept 28 (Reuters) - Mexico extended its dollar auction program to defend the peso on Monday for another two months amid a spike in global volatility that drove the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a record low last week.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission said in a statement it would extend its current dollar auction regime, without any changes, through the end of November.\par \par \uc2 While Brazil stepped up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts last week, \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reluctant to fight the market due to its commitment to a freely floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The peso reversed early losses to gain a slight 0.1 percent after the announcement. It has been punished this year amid a rout in emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Policymakers from the finance ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement that they were watching global markets to see if they needed to adjust the program, which sells up to $400 million a day and was set to expire on Sept. 30.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll forecast Mexico would hold the program steady without changes.\par \par \uc2 Since starting dollar auctions in December, Mexico's reserves have fallen by about 5 percent to around $183 billion as the country sold more than $15 billion. (Reporting by Veronica Gomez and Michael O'Boyle; Editing by Meredith Mazzilli) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-28T15:43:03.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument L