\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-\uc2 Mexico  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  split over possible hike before the Fed\b0\par\par\uc2 482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2015\par 18:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analyst comments)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Aug 13 (Reuters) - \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was divided over its decision in July to hold rates steady, with one member arguing that a pre-emptive hike before the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  acts would help ease uncertainty that has hit financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The bank board voted 3 to 1 at its July 30 meeting to hold the benchmark rate at a record-low 3 percent, minutes released on Thursday showed. Board member Manuel Ramos Francia was not present for the decision.\par \par \uc2 In agreeing to keep the rate unchanged, the majority said the economy was still weak, while inflation expectations had not been hurt by deep losses in the peso.\par \par \uc2 The one dissenting member, who voted in favor of a quarter-point hike, said that a "preventive" move was convenient, given imminent rate action expected from the Fed. The minutes do not say how each member present at the board meeting voted.\par \par \uc2 Some members said a hike before the Fed could be justified if Mexican financial stability was threatened by a mass sell-off of peso bonds by foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 "They are probably sending us a signal that they are ready to act if things get ugly," said \uc2 Marco Oviedo\uc2 , an analyst at Barcalys Capital in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Banco de Mexico Governor \uc2 Agustin Carstens\uc2  on Wednesday said policymakers could act before the Fed if peso losses threaten inflation expectations. Major businesses have warned they may have to raise prices if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to slump.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  could increase \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the peso hits 17 per dollar, about 4 percent weaker from current levels, and a disorderly move past that level could prompt the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten policy, according to a Reuters poll on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 A majority of the members thought a timely adjustment of rates, as well as further improvements in Mexico's fiscal stance, were needed to maintain investor confidence in the country.\par \par \uc2 The government cut spending this year after global oil prices tumbled, curbing income from state-run oil company \uc2 Pemex\uc2 . The Finance Ministry will soon send the 2016 budget to Congress.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso has weakened to record lows this year over concerns that higher U.S. borrowing costs will drive investors to dump emerging-market assets.\par \par \uc2 The majority of board members at the July 30 policy meeting said the inflation rate, which hit a record low for a third consecutive month in July, would stay below the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 3 percent target for the rest of the year given the slack in the economy.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday cuts its growth outlook for this year on weak exports and a slump in domestic oil output. (Reporting by Michael O'Boyle and Alexandra Alper; Editing by Jonathan Oatis and Alan Crosby) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-13T17:36:03.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150813eb8d00i36\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
