\par\par \b \uc2 POLL- \highlight22\uc2 Mexico central bank \highlight\uc2  may  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  again at 17 pesos per dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2015\par 19:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details on analysts forecasts, background on peso development)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Aug 12 (Reuters) - Mexico could further bolster its defense of the peso if it hits 17 per dollar and a disorderly move past that level could push the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates, according to the median forecast of a Reuters poll.\par \par \uc2 Seven of 13 analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could strengthen its program of dollar sales if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits around 17 per dollar, about 4 percent weaker from current levels. Reuters polled 16 analysts in total, but three refused to name a level, saying the bank is not defending a specific exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Five of the analysts said the bank could move even earlier and increase \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the peso breaks past 16.50 again. One said 16.80 could trigger more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The peso traded flat on Wednesday around 16.3150 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Analysts also couched their answer by saying the chance of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would depend how fast the peso hit 17.\par \par \uc2 A bigger move by the peso compared to other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would raise the chance of stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while a depreciation that is in line with other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would lessen the chance of more dollar sales.\par \par \uc2 Late last month, Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission raised daily dollar auctions to $200 million from $52 million to support the peso after the peso hit a record low of 16.49 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The commission also lowered the threshold of peso losses that can trigger the sale of an additional $200 million per day.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also warned that it could raise interest rates at any time, if needed to keep inflation around its 3 percent target.\par \par \uc2 Four of 10 analysts said 17 per dollar could trigger an interest rate hike. Two said 17.10, one 17.20, another 17.50, while two said 16.80. Six of the 16 did not answer with a level and doubted the bank would move before the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mexican inflation has cooled to a record low for the last three months, but policymakers have said they are watching for signs a more than 20 percent slump in the peso since last July could lead to higher inflation expectations. (Reporting by Jean Luis Arce, Noe Torres and Michael O'Boyle; Editing by David Gregorio)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-15T17:50:43.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150812eb8c00oox\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
