\par\par \b \uc2 Weak Mexican peso stokes price pressure, shakes up  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael O'Boyle   \par \uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2015\par 16:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Aug 3 (Reuters) - Mexican companies warn they will have to raise prices if a steep slide in the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  persists, adding pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hike interest rates despite a weak economy.\par \par \uc2 With economic growth running at only slightly above 2 percent, business leaders have mainly held back from passing the increased costs of imported goods and materials on to consumers, but some now say they cannot continue to bear the full burden.\par \par \uc2 "If the exchange rate stays at 16 per dollar or weaker, in the next two months we'll surely need to make some adjustment, because it won't be possible to keep absorbing these types of shocks," said Juan Casados, general director of Concamin, a confederation representing Mexican industry associations.\par \par \uc2 Price hikes by manufacturers could push service providers to follow suit, unleash demand for higher wages and undo Mexico's success in bringing down inflation.\par \par \uc2 The peso has fallen by about 20 percent against the dollar since last July and is the 10th worst-performing among the world's 36 most-traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, Reuters data shows.\par \par \uc2 Eight record peso lows in July alone reawakened memories of past devaluations that caused havoc to Mexico's economy.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Federal Reserve may start raising rates in September and Mexico is likely to quickly follow. That may help the peso stabilize, but it may not happen soon enough.\par \par \uc2 Marcello Hinojosa, who runs a waste-recycling firm in the northern border city of Tijuana and imports about 30 percent of his inputs, says his business had been shouldering higher import costs since September.\par \par \uc2 "Now it's so much that we're more than 20 percent higher and we can't absorb the total cost of this increase," said Hinojosa, the head of a local chapter of lobby group Canacintra, which represents industries ranging from food and drink, chemicals, construction, home furnishings and other manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 "We're going to have to raise prices," he added.\par \par \uc2 Business leaders should be careful about stoking inflationary expectations, said Banamex economist Sergio Luna.\par \par \uc2 "There is a psychological element in all of this," he said, noting financial authorities would be following warnings from business leaders about price pressures "very closely."\par \par \uc2 Tepid demand should prevent any sharp moves though, say economists. Growth has fallen short of forecasts for three years running.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Importers have already passed on some cost increases. Annual inflation for non-food goods rose to 2.65 percent in early July, up 86 basis points from the same month last year.\par \par \uc2 Over that period, annual inflation cooled to a record low of 2.76 percent as services cost pressures eased, helped by lower phone bills following a telecommunications reform and a decline in some commodity prices that outpaced the peso's fall.\par \par \uc2 Those dampening effects will fade by next year, and if the peso remains weak, rising costs could push inflation back above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 3 percent target.\par \par \uc2 Still, any price increases should only reflect a fraction of the depreciation because companies can avoid passing on local costs set in pesos, including distribution, said \uc2 Armando Garza Sada\uc2 , chairman of conglomerate Alfa.\par \par \uc2 "Normally, if it ends up being lasting, a devaluation of 10 percent, prices could rise between 2 and 3 percent," he said. "I'm sure the \uc2 Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't let the devaluation hit inflation."\par \par \uc2 Policymakers \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  late last week, when \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission nearly quadrupled the sum auctioned to support the peso to $200 million a day and lowered the threshold that can trigger the sale of $200 million more.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Agustin Carstens\uc2  then said on Friday that policymakers could raise rates at any time, even before the Fed if the peso needs the support.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted the peso off Thursday's record low, but it is still above 16 per dollar, in terrain that was previously uncharted before late July.\par \par \uc2 Despite the support, traders could try to drive down the peso to test the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's willingness to hike rates.\par \par \uc2 "The governor is making all the right noises. As he should, given the volatility," said Andrew Stanners, a fund manager at \uc2 Aberdeen Asset Management\uc2  in London. "But clearly the macro backdrop and current low pass through means the market could test that conviction." (Additional reporting by Alexandra Alper, Lizbeth Diaz, Joanna Zuckerman Bernstein, Anna Yukhananov, Jean Luis Arce; Editing by Dave Graham and Kieran Murray)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-6T14:35:07.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150803eb8300hza\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
