\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2 's finmin says peso's recovery due to  \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 307 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 August 2015\par 04:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quote, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission details, and peso's Friday close)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, July 31 (Reuters) - The Mexican peso recovered on Friday in response to the government's recent reinforcement of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, Finance Minister Luis Videgaray said in a radio interview.\par \par \uc2 Videgaray added that statements from the governor of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the day also helped bolster the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The peso strengthened nearly 1 percent on Friday to 16.1135 per dollar, just one day after sinking to a historic low of 16.49 per greenback.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission, run jointly by the finance ministry and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , significantly boosted daily dollar auctions on Thursday in an effort to protect the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Specifically, the commission raised daily dollar auctions to $200 million from $52 million to support the peso, while also lowering the threshold of peso losses that can trigger the sale of an additional $200 million per day.\par \par \uc2 The peso's rally on Friday, its largest daily gain in more than 11 weeks, "has to do with the measures the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission has taken... and secondly, I also think the market has begun to understand the position of the \uc2 Banco de Mexico\uc2 ," said Videgaray.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Agustin Carstens\uc2  said earlier on Friday that the bank could raise interest rates at any time to defend the peso, independently of what the U.S. Federal Reserve does.\par \par \uc2 The peso has fallen by about 20 percent against the dollar since last July, and several record lows this month alone reawakened memories of past devaluations that caused havoc to the country's economy. (Reporting by Jean Luis Arce; Writing by David Alire Garcia; Editing by Ken Wills) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-1T03:20:37.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150801eb81001xh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
