\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico's Carstens says peso slump 'exaggerated' -report\b0\par\par\uc2 306 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2015\par 15:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, July 3 (Reuters) - The slump in the Mexican peso to a record low this week has been "exaggerated" by global volatility but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should bounce back due to the outlook for Latin America's No. 2 economy, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor \uc2 Agustin Carstens\uc2  told the state news agency.\par \par \uc2 Carstens, in an interview with Notimex late Thursday, said that the peso's slump "could be a transitory phenomenon" related to global volatility and that Mexico's solid fundamentals should support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso has tumbled more than 15 percent since late last year on a collapse in world oil prices and bets that higher U.S. interest rates will draw investors out of emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the peso hit a record low 15.8420 per dollar. Mexican inflation has continued to cool this year to a record low below 3 percent despite the peso's losses and its impact on import prices as a sluggish economy contains price pressures.\par \par \uc2 Carstens said monetary authorities, which include the finance ministry, thought the peso market was operating in an orderly manner and with sufficient liquidity, Notimex said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  avoids direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but analysts at Banorte said in a note Thursday that Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission could increase the amount of dollars it sells in auctions or take other measures to try and slow the peso's slump if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breaks past 16 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Carstens said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was better prepared to face volatility due to any moves by the U.S. Federal Reserve after Mexico this week changed its own calendar of interest rate decisions to more closely follow upcoming Fed meetings, Notimex reported.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Michael O'Boyle; Editing by Andrea Ricci) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-7-3T14:39:08.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150703eb7300eig\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
