\par\par \b \uc2 Steeper  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  Peso Decline Could Prompt  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,417 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2015\par 21:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 15:49 EDT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission, formed by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government officials, could take further measures to support the tumbling peso, Banorte analysts say. The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a record low against the US dollar this week, and recently traded around 15.67 to the greenback. "A quick depreciation near or above 16 pesos per dollar could convince them to act." The bank says the commission could increase the amount of dollars offered in daily auctions to $100M from the current $52M. (juan.montes@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:44 EDT - The percent of US farm acres planted with GMO corn and cotton declined this year from 2014, according to data released by \uc2 USDA\uc2  this week, reflecting tough financial times for farmers who have had to scrutinize spending. In some cases farmers have dumped higher-priced genetically engineered seeds for cheaper non-GMO varieties, while others gravitated toward non-GMO crops in search of premium prices paid for the crops by food companies. Biotech's still popular: this year 92% of corn acres planted with GMO seed, down from 93% last year, but that step back represents its first decline in 15 years, according to \uc2 USDA\uc2 . GMO cotton dips to 94% from 96% of planted acres and biotech soybeans remain on 94% of acres, steady from last year. (jacob.bunge@wsj.com; @jacobbunge)\par \par \uc2 15:41 EDT - The bourbon boom fueled a new record in 2014 as inventory of aging barrels hit 5.7 million, a 40-year high, according to the Kentucky Distillers' Association. The inventory increases come as global thirst for bourbon has forced distillers like \uc2 Beam Suntory\uc2 , \uc2 Diageo\uc2  (DEO) and \uc2 Brown-Forman\uc2  (BF.B) to ratchet up production. The Kentucky Distillers' Association said production topped 1.3 million barrels last year, a more than 170% increase from 1999 when 485,020 barrels were put away. The tax-assessed value of aging barrels in Kentucky is $2.1B. (tripp.mickle@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:38 EDT - Brazil's industrial production posts its first positive print in four months in May, but the result doesn't seem to be indicative of a nascent recovery of the industrial sector, according to \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economist Alberto Ramos. Brazil's industrial production grew 0.6% on month in May, snapping three consecutive months of negative results. But \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  notes that the industrial sector has been reducing headcount in Brazil and that it continues to face strong headwinds from high inventories, very low confidence indicators, rising tax burden and energy costs, and weak external demand, particularly from Argentina for durable goods. (luciana.magalhaes@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:34 EDT - Michigan Governor \uc2 Rick Snyder\uc2  today appoints a financial review team to delve into the finances of Wayne County after a preliminary review found deteriorating conditions, including ballooning debt, dwindling revenue and underfunded pensions. The appointment of a review team is the second step that could end in a consent agreement between the county and the state; takeover by an state-appointed emergency manager; or Chapter 9 bankruptcy filing. State officials said their initial review found that without taking remedial measures, the county which includes the city of Detroit is projecting a $171.4M deficit by fiscal 2019. (matthew.dolan@wsj.com; @matthewsdolan)\par \par \uc2 15:28 EDT - The flat wage reading bolsters the case for the Fed to wait longer than September to raise rates, which has energized the short end of the bond market and steepened the yield curve. The 30-year bond is the underperformer as investors are moving cash to short end. The benchmark yield curve--the 2yr/10yr yield spread--widened to 175.6 bps from 172.8bps Wednesday. The curve is trading just a touch below the steepest level since November made in June. Some traders say if yields at short end are anchored here, it would keep a lid on how high 10-year yield can rise in the coming months. (min.zeng@wsj.com;@minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 15:25 EDT - The larger emerging eastern European nations of Poland, Czech Republic and Hungary have little financial or economic ties to Greece. But Greek-owned banks are riddled across some of the smaller developing markets, such as Bulgaria, where foreign claims by Greek banks stand at 19% of GDP, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . In Macedonia, that's 17% of GPD, 15% in Albania and 8% in Romania. "In a scenario where parent banks go bankrupt and raid assets [or] are forced to sell local subsidiaries, this could trigger government bailouts with a resulting impact on the sovereign balance sheet," DB says. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 15:22 EDT - Each of Och-Ziff's (OZM) hedge funds had declines last month, a difficult period for the sector in general amid broad stock pullbacks. OZM's flagship fund lost an estimated 0.5%, putting its 1H return at 4.1%, the company said in its monthly \uc2 SEC\uc2  filing on fund performance. The firm's Asia and Europe funds, shed 0.9% and 1%, respectively, lowering their 2015 returns to 12% and 5.2%. Meanwhile, assets under management fell some $1.3B from the start of the month to $46.8B. Jefferies says that means OZM had a 2Q net outflow of as much as $1B. It adds the Master Fund "continued to demonstrate downside protection" as it held up better than the \uc2 S&P 500\uc2 , which fell 2%. OZM is down 0.2% today and 3.8% for the week. (juliet.chung@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:11 EDT - \uc2 BP\uc2  will likely be able to deduct the bulk of its $18.7B settlement on its taxes, so the settlement isn't as big as it seems, says the US Public Interest Research Group. Only $5.5B of the settlement with the Justice Department regarding the 2010 Deepwater Horizon explosion is classified as a penalty, which can't be deducted. But the remainder can be deemed ordinary business expenses unless there's language in the settlement agreement specifically barring that. Thus, the company's tax bill could be cut by billions. The interest group is calling on \uc2 BP\uc2  to pledge it won't take a tax benefit from the settlement payments. \uc2 BP\uc2  couldn't immediately be reached for comment. (michael.rapoport@wsj.com; @rapoportwsj)\par \par \uc2 14:59 EDT - Lumber contracts rebound slightly after slipping to a new four-week low in the prior session, with front-month July contract settling Thursday at $288.50 per 1,000 board feet, up 50 cents, or 0.2%. Most-active September contract adds a buck, closing at $285.70. CME-listed lumber futures have fallen about 3% from mid-June levels, but remain far above where they were in early May, when they sunk to their lowest in 3.5 years. (jacob.bunge@wsj.com; @jacobbunge)\par \par \uc2 14:48 EDT - The July 1 expiration of the Export-Import bank charter likely means more company-backed financing on deals for \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's (BA) jetliners, say company executives, but the bank might be back in a hurry if times turn tough. The plane maker has relied less and less on the bank to support sales as capital is more easily available and export credit costs have increased. The use of the bank by \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's customers has fallen from its 2011 peak supporting 30% of deliveries down to 12% in 2014. "We'd expect that the impact of the bank's absence will be relatively benign in the short/medium term," writes \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's Myles Walton, but if a liquidity crisis sets in "we'd expect Congress would quickly reinstate the charter and come around to understanding the merits of having the bank in place." (jon.ostrower@wsj.com; @jonostrower)\par \par \uc2 14:41 EDT - The world's largest money manager says despite the setback in wage growth, the solid recovery in the US is the real story. Rick Rieder, CIO of fundamental fixed income at BlackRock, says June's jobs data on balance exceeds the reasonable measures a data-dependent Fed might require to raise rates. He admits that "the headline risks stemming from the Greece situation/turmoil in Europe may well push the Fed back from increasing policy rates in September, particularly if aggressive \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  easing leads to further dollar strength." (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 02, 2015 15:49 ET (19:49 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150702eb720032u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
