\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Mexican likely to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  more if peso slumps further\b0\par\par\uc2 416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2015\par 20:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, May 20 (Reuters) - \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to take stronger measures to support the peso if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits a new low in the coming months, according to analysts polled by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Four of seven economists queried at a round table hosted by Reuters in Mexico City expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to introduce bolder forms of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the peso blows past a record low of 15.6690 per dollar seen in March.\par \par \uc2 Sergio Luna, an economist at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  in Mexico City, said policymakers are committed to a free floating exchange rate, but that they are thinking "if things happen, I reserve the right to take out my missiles and show them off a bit."\par \par \uc2 The peso has been hammered by concerns that a U.S. interest rate hike may push investors to dump emerging market assets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  avoids the direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  used by other emerging markets, preferring more market-friendly mechanisms, such as a current auction of $200 million dollars that is triggered by losses of more than 1.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Analysts doubted policymakers would resort to discretionary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , expecting rather that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could increase the amount of dollars it sells or lower the threshold that would trigger the auction.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the finance ministry jointly set \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 Despite the chance for deeper \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses, all economists saw only a minimal sell-off of peso-denominated bonds. Six out of seven project a drop of around 10 percent in the holdings of foreign investors. One expects foreign holdings to remain stable.\par \par \uc2 Foreign holdings of Mexican peso-debt have dipped about two percent so far off a record high of 2.18 trillion pesos ($143 billion) seen in February.\par \par \uc2 Even though Mexican growth has been slower than expected, economists said the country's relatively strong fundamentals made it stand out from other emerging markets suffering from domestic problems, such as Brazil and Russia.\par \par \uc2 "As one client put it, 'It's a beauty pageant and Mexico continues to be, by far, the least ugly,'" said Alexis Milo, an economist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  slashed its growth outlook for this year on Tuesday by half a percentage point to between 2.0-3.0 percent. ($1 = 15.2060 Mexican pesos) (Reporting by Michael O'Boyle and Jean Luis Arce; Editing by Christian Plumb)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-5-23T18:53:43.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150520eb5k00t0h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
