\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Leaves Door Open to Act Before or After Fed\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amy Guthrie   \par \uc2 473 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 18:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ACAPULCO, Mexico--\uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  Gov. \uc2 Agustin Carstens\uc2  said on Friday he would like to keep options open to adjust monetary policy either before or after an expected interest-rate increase by the U.S. Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 Ideally, the \uc2 Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be able to observe both the Fed's decision and its impact on markets before making a move itself, Mr. Carstens told a group of reporters at a Mexican bankers convention.\par \par \uc2 But if other factors merit preemptive action, then the bank might also consider the possibility of moving at a different time, he said. \uc2 The Fed\uc2  is widely expected to start raising interest rates sometime this year.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is expected to start raising interest rates once the Fed does, kept its overnight lending rate target at a record-low 3% at its January meeting. The next policy meeting is scheduled for March 26.\par \par \uc2 Annual inflation in Mexico slowed to 3%--the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official target--at the end of February, helped by lower electricity and telephone service costs.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though, has seen sharp swings recently as the market anticipates Fed action after years of lax monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso touched a record-low against the U.S. dollar last week of 15.67 pesos. The peso was gaining Friday, trading in Mexico City around 15.00 pesos to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Carstens said that while the bank prefers transparent mechanisms such as pre-scheduled dollar auctions to stabilize the peso, he wouldn't rule out the possibility of direct dollar sales in the market if there is a drastic move in the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 "It would really have to be an extraordinary case," he said, adding that any direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be temporary.\par \par \uc2 Since March 11, the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling $52 million a day via auctions to help curtail volatility in the exchange market. The bank also offers $200 million on days when the peso weakens 1.5% or more from the previous session. The conditional auctions have only triggered dollar sales twice since they were activated in early December.\par \par \uc2 Like many emerging markets, Mexico has benefited in recent years from an influx of foreign capital as investors seek higher yields due to low interest rates in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Mexican authorities are doing what they can to retain that capital and attract more, if possible, Mr. Carstens said.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican government, which is highly dependent on oil revenue for its budget, has reduced spending plans this year. It has also been active in overseas debt markets to extend debt maturities.\par \par \uc2 Write to Amy Guthrie at Amy.guthrie@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 20, 2015 13:42 ET (17:42 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150320eb3k002s2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
