\par\par \b \uc2 China Reportedly Tells Coal Companies to Cut Back -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,385 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 18:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 13:32 EDT - Is coal production slowing in China? Possibly. According to a note by Investec, citing the Chinese press, 17 coal companies have been ordered to cut production after a recent development and reform commission in China asked for the move. The unconfirmed cutbacks would come a year after annual Chinese coal production fell for the first time since 2000. The shift would be good news for mining-giant \uc2 Glencore\uc2 , which derives about one-quarter of its mining revenue from coal. Still, coal supplies remain well in excess of demand, Investec notes. "Unfortunately, it is likely that it will take years for supply-demand imbalance to even out," it said. (scott.patterson@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:13 EDT - \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor \uc2 Agustin Carstens\uc2  says the bank will consider direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if swings in the peso become too dramatic. "It would really have to be an extraordinary case," he says, adding that direct USD sales in the market would be temporary. The Mexican peso, which touched a record-low against the US dollar last week of MXN15.67, is trading in Mexico City around MXN15.00/USD. (Amy.guthrie@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:08 EDT - The dollar's slide today is turning into a bonanza for US grain futures. May-dated \uc2 CBOT\uc2  wheat contracts surge 3.5% to $5.29 3/4 a bushel, hitting the contracts' highest level in a month as the greenback weakens. Along for the ride is corn, with May contracts up 2.8% to $3.84 a bushel, and soybeans, up 1.7% to $9.78 1/4 a bushel in midday trade. The US dollar, which impacts competitiveness of US crops on international markets, recently down 1.3% versus a basket of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked by the WSJ Dollar Index. (jacob.bunge@wsj.com; @jacobbunge)\par \par \uc2 13:08 EDT - \uc2 Barclays\uc2  cuts its GDP views on Latin America's 2 largest countries as "falling commodity prices, the looming US tightening cycle and idiosyncratic political and environmental shocks look set to make 2015 a difficult year" for the region. The investment bank now sees Brazil's economy contracting 1.1% this year, compared with a 0.8% expansion expected previously, while its growth view on Mexico drops to 2.6% from 3.3%. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:07 EDT - Investors in Egypt stand to gain from two trends that are driving growth in the country's economy, says Hedi Ben Mlouka, chief investment officer of Duet Group's $130M global frontier market fund. First is the country's recovery from what Mr. Mlouka calls a failed democratic transition. "The economy has suffered a lot in the past few years. Foreign investors have shied away because of the perceived risks so valuations have dropped quite a lot. We see a lot of upside," he told WSJ Frontiers. Mr. Mlouka is also excited about the government's reform plans: "We don't believe all of them will be done but the marginal reforms will have a dramatic impact." (dan.keeler@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:02 EDT - \uc2 The Fed\uc2  may no longer be "patient" about raising rates, but its downgraded economic projections has \uc2 Barclays\uc2  thinking it won't hike until September. The investment bank previously forecast a June liftoff. Specifically, it says the lower view on long-term unemployment suggests policy makers still see slack left in the labor market which can be absorbed with the help of easy policy. The onus is on labor indicators the next 2 months to show an acceleration in jobs and wage-growth pickup to make a June rate hike a possibility. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 12:21 EDT - A strong 2.1% increase in Mexican retail sales in January from December gives cause for optimism after a disappointing industrial production report for the month, "providing a balanced outlook for economic activity this quarter, although we remain cautious," says \uc2 Barclays\uc2  economist \uc2 Marco Oviedo\uc2 . While more data are needed to confirm a recovery, supermarket and department store sales seem to be exiting the stagnation observed in 2013 and 2014, he adds. Seasonally adjusted retail sales were also up 4.9% from January 2014. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:53 EDT - Employers complain frequently they can't find workers matching the requirements for jobs, but new research confirms what observers have long suspected: employers opportunistically raise the credential requirements listed in job ads in response to an oversupply of job-seekers. Increases in the number of people looking for work accounts for roughly 30% of the total increase in employers' skill requirements, according to economists at Northeastern and \uc2 Harvard\uc2  Universities and the \uc2 Boston Fed\uc2 , which reviewed a large database of online job ads posted from 2007-10. They also found the wage premium paid to recent college graduates declines as unemployment rises since employers believe they can hire educated workers more cheaply. "Our results indicate that the demand for skilled workers is perhaps more dynamic and responsive to labor-market conditions than previously thought." (lauren.weber@wsj.com; @laurenweberWSJ)\par \par \uc2 11:24 EDT - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says the $60/barrel drop in oil prices since June was roughly $25 supply-driven, $10 demand-driven and $25 driven by a drop in long-dated prices. That means if later-dated prices hadn't adjusted due to scalability and cost deflation in shale-oil production, Brent crude prices should only have fallen to about $80/barrel, the bank says. "The entire industry is re-pricing as costs decline and further efficiency gains are made...The biggest single event that occurred in 2014 was the recognition of the scalability of shale and the true size of the resource base." Goldman sees the new equilibrium price for oil at $70 Brent and $65 WTI. Brent up 1.3% at $55.15/barrel. WTI up 4.2% at $45.80/barrel. (nicole.friedman@wsj.com; @NicoleFriedman)\par \par \uc2 11:03 EDT - National Bank Financial says there's an important event for Canadian markets next week: Thursday's speech and press conference by Bank of Canada Gov. Poloz. The firm notes after using some very-dovish language to support his surprise decision to cut rates in January, Poloz quickly changed his tune to support a no-change decision earlier this month. "Another U-turn won't help his credibility, and as such we expect him to stick to" the latest message. The OIS market is pricing in another rate cut in the coming months, and it will be interesting to see whether or not Poloz reinforces such expectations. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 11:03 EDT -- Focus in Colombia today is on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and if it'll raise its key interest rate or keep it steady at 4.5% as it has for the past six months. A rate hike would help control inflation, which has swelled close to the bank's maximum target of 4%. But the bank has to balance inflationary concerns with the country's economic slowdown amid lower oil prices. Officials have welcomed the weakening of the Colombian peso in recent months, and likely won't want to disrupt that with a raise in rates. Analysts at Credicorp Capital says the rate decision depends largely on whether policymakers see the rise in inflation as temporary or a lasting trend. (sara.munoz@wsj.com; @sarasmunoz)\par \par \uc2 10:48 EDT - \uc2 The Fed\uc2 's surprisingly lower Fed-funds projections may not stem entirely from the weight of a stronger dollar. Capital Economics suspects policy makers still have the rate surge during the mid-2013 "taper tantrum" fresh in their minds, which "probably explains why the Fed is now falling over itself to stress how slowly its policy rate will rise." If that message doesn't sink in with market participants, the first rate hike, however well broadcast, could cause a bond selloff that risks dampening the economic recovery and delaying further tightening. CapEcon says the Fed should bite the bullet sooner, allowing more breathing room to make subsequent hikes very gradually. It expects a June liftoff. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 20, 2015 13:32 ET (17:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150320eb3k002oh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
