\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-\uc2 Mexican  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  to restart dollar auctions amid peso slump\b0\par\par\uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2014\par 22:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds economist's comment, poll data on inflation expectations)\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle and Luis Rojas\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Dec 8 (Reuters) - \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will restart a dollar auction program on Tuesday to help cushion the peso after a sharp slump in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has threatened to push inflation higher.\par \par \uc2 The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission said in a statement on Monday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will offer $200 million a day at an auction that would be triggered whenever the peso has shed more than 1.5 percent from the previous session's fix rate.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso has tumbled about 11 percent since June, hitting a 2-1/2-year low on Friday. Last week the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the peso's steep slide, if prolonged, could begin to push up inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefly reversed losses after the announcement but then eased to trade about 0.3 percent weaker at 14.40 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 In an interview published on Monday in El Universal, a daily newspaper, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor \uc2 Agustin Carstens\uc2  suggested the bank may have to raise interest rates if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's losses affect consumer prices.\par \par \uc2 "If they raise interest rates they are going to make the government's financing more expensive, so I think they will try to avoid that," said Benito Berber, an analyst at Nomura in New York. "But they might not be able to avoid it, if inflation expectations move up."\par \par \uc2 The median forecast for inflation next year is 3.41 percent, according to a Banamex poll of analysts issued last week.\par \par \uc2 Data on Tuesday is expected to show that Mexico's annual inflation rate fell to 4.19 percent in November from 4.30 percent in October.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday said it expected inflation to fall to around 3 percent by the middle of next year.\par \par \uc2 Unlike other emerging market countries that favor direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  is committed to a flexible exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 The auction program is seen as a more market-friendly form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it operates by clear rules.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly set \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, but the finance ministry casts the deciding vote.\par \par \uc2 Mexico last activated the dollar auction program in 2011 and suspended it in April 2013. At that time it offered $400 million a day in an auction that was triggered by a 2 percent slide.\par \par \uc2 Analysts noted that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could bump up the allotted amount for auctions if the peso continued to weaken sharply. (Reporting by Michael O'Boyle and Jean Luis Arce; Editing by Simon Gardner and Paul Simao)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-12-11T21:03:43.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141208eac800ngh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
