\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican Peso Hits 2 1/2-Year Low Against Dollar; Stocks Down\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Harrup   \par \uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2014\par 22:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY--Mexico's peso lost more ground Tuesday against the U.S. dollar as oil prices resumed their slide, and local stocks fell for a third straight session.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IPC\uc2  index of most-traded stocks fell 0.9% to 43,008 points on volume of 195.9 million shares worth 7.18 billion pesos ($509 million). The peso slid to its weakest level against the dollar since June of 2012, closing in Mexico City at MXN14.1220, according to Infosel, compared with MXN13.9865 on Monday.\par \par \uc2 "Unlike previous months, when the peso resisted dollar appreciation more than other economies, in November it was affected by the swift drop in crude oil prices," the Ve Por Mas bank said in a note. "Secondly, economic indicators aren't showing a solid recovery."\par \par \uc2 Economists surveyed last month by the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  lowered their growth estimate for this year to 2.2% from 2.3%, still within the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's estimated range of 2% to 2.5%, while the median estimate for 2015 growth fell to 3.5% from 3.7%. The estimate for the exchange rate at the end of the year moved to MXN13.50 to the dollar from MXN13.32 in October's survey.\par \par \uc2 Nomura strategist Benito Berber said the peso's move above 14 to the dollar for the first time in two-and-a-half years wasn't that much of a problem as long as the market remains orderly with liquidity, although the recent weakening looks overextended. He said he expects the exchange rate to be "closer to 13.50 than 14" in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 Scotiabank cut its year-end estimate for the peso to MXN14.01 from MXN13.40, and also lowered its Mexican growth estimates for this year and next.\par \par \uc2 While crude oil at current prices accounts for less than 10% of Mexico's overall exports, if prices stay low for an extended period it could affect government revenue and possibly appetite for Mexico's round of bidding for oil blocks, the first in almost eight decades thanks to recent changes in the country's energy laws.\par \par \uc2 "Some of these projects, such as deep waters, have higher production costs than surface wells, which at current prices could be unprofitable and slow down to some extent the flow of investment expected from the energy reform," Scotiabank said. "An important part of the problem is that it isn't clear at what level prices could stabilize."\par \par \uc2 The selling Tuesday extended to Mexican government bonds, pushing yields higher for most maturities. The yield on actively traded 10-year bonds due 2024 rose 17 basis points to 5.91%.\par \par \uc2 As of Monday, Mexican authorities weren't planning to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market to stem the decline in the peso.\par \par \uc2 "As long as the exchange market is orderly, and has liquidity, as it clearly does, the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Finance Ministry won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market because, at the end of the day, the price of the dollar is determined by by the market, by supply and demand," Finance Minister Luis Videgaray said in a local radio interview Monday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission is run by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Finance Ministry.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anthony Harrup at anthony.harrup@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 02, 2014 16:43 ET (21:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141202eac2003wh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
