\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2 's finance minister rules out  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 292 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 2014\par 19:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds minister's comments on lower taxes)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, Dec 1 (Reuters) - Mexico's finance minister told local radio on Monday that the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is orderly and he does not see a need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso slumped to a 2-1/2 year intraday low on Monday before recovering somewhat.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shed more than 3 percent last month amid concerns about higher interest rates in the United States and a sharp drop in oil prices. Mexico is one of the top crude exporters to the United States.\par \par \uc2 "As long as the market is ordered, has liquidity, as it clearly does, the authority ... will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market," Finance Minister Luis Videgaray said in a radio interview.\par \par \uc2 Unlike other emerging market countries that favor \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  is committed to a flexible exchange rate. The finance ministry and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly set \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, but the ministry casts the deciding vote.\par \par \uc2 Videgaray also said the government was not opposed to lowering taxes eventually if economic conditions allowed.\par \par \uc2 Videgaray previously pledged not to raise taxes through the rest of his term after he pushed through increases for this year that have been blamed for crimping growth.\par \par \uc2 "We have never said that taxes should not be lowered," Videgaray said. "We are not opposed to discussing this."\par \par \uc2 Mexican lawmakers have already approved tax codes for next year, and Videgaray said any discussion about lower taxes would have to take place once the framework for 2016 is looked at, which will happen in the later part of next year. (Reporting by Ana Isabel Martinez. Editing by Andre Grenon) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-12-1T19:31:38.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141201eac100m34\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
