\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil real up on finmin talk; \uc2 America Movil\uc2  sinks Mexico index\b0\par\par\uc2 300 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2014\par 17:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Nov 12 (Reuters) - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened on Wednesday amid market chatter that President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2  was strongly considering a market-friendly name for finance minister.\par \par \uc2 The MSCI Latin American stock index rose for the first session in three, led by Brazilian stocks, although Mexican shares fell their most in a week.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real erased the previous session's losses after traders began to speculate that Rousseff is strongly considering \uc2 Henrique Meirelles\uc2 , a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president and market favorite, for the finance minister post.\par \par \uc2 Her decision has been the market's primary focus since she won re-election last month.\par \par \uc2 "The market is quite sensitive about any signal about economic policy for the coming years," said Reginaldo Galhardo, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at brokerage Treviso in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's Bovespa stock index posted its biggest gain this month as investors picked up shares of banks and state-run enterprises, both of which have suffered in the past due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Rousseff's government.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \uc2 IPC\uc2  stock index slid for the fifth straight day, led by a more than 2 percent drop in shares of heavily weighted telecommunications company \uc2 America Movil\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The stock fell after \uc2 AT&T\uc2 's chief executive officer said on Wednesday that the company did not need to acquire assets of \uc2 America Movil\uc2  to succeed in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Chile's IPSA stock index rose its most in more than a week, led by a gain of more than 3 percent gain in retailer Falabella's shares.\par \par \uc2 The company said earnings rose about 6 percent in the third quarter. (Reporting by Bruno Federowski; Writing by Asher Levine; Editing by Lisa Von Ahn) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-11-12T17:57:21.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141112eabc00kid\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
