\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-\uc2 Mexico  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  says inflation has likely peaked\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael O'Boyle and Tomas Sarmiento   \par \uc2 513 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2014\par 22:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Feb 12 (Reuters) - \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday said that a recent spike in inflation had likely peaked, suggesting policymakers will leave interest rates steady this year.\par \par \uc2 Banco de Mexico Governor \uc2 Agustin Carstens\uc2  said at the presentation of the bank's quarterly report that policymakers would act if they saw that a recent jump in consumer prices was spurring wider price pressures, such as higher wages.\par \par \uc2 Consumer price inflation in Latin America's No. 2 economy rose to an eight-month high of 4.48 percent in January due mostly to a new tax on soft drinks, surging past the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's limit of 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The shocks we are seeing are transitory," Carstens said. "We would raise interest rates if we saw evidence of second-round effects."\par \par \uc2 Carstens said inflation had likely peaked during the first half of January, when the new taxes took effect, and price behavior in the second part of the month suggested the tax hike was not spurring wider price pressures.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in its report that annual inflation would remain above 4 percent in the first months of 2014 before falling below that level in the second half of the year. Last November, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecast inflation of around 3.5 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its main interest rate on hold at a record low 3.50 percent on Jan. 31, saying it would watch inflation expectations while also making sure a slump in the peso does not stoke further price pressures.\par \par \uc2 The recent rise in inflation had prompted bets in the interest rate swap market for a hike this year. A Banamex poll last week showed the median estimate is for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates in March 2015.\par \par \uc2 NO SELL-OFF\par \par \uc2 Carstens said there was little chance that \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would resort to discretionary \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market amid a global slump in emerging market assets.\par \par \uc2 The country's peso is down more than 2 percent this year, but \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eschewed the forms of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  used by other emerging economies to prop up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Carstens also said he doubted there would be a "selloff" of Mexican assets since the Mexican economy was stronger than other emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Foreign holdings of Mexican peso debt have grown nearly seven-fold since the end of 2008 to more than 1.8 trillion Mexican pesos ($135 billion) and a mass exit by foreigners would hammer the peso.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the economy grew around 1.2 percent in 2013 and it stuck to its forecast for an expansion in 2014 of between 3.0 percent and 4.0 percent.\par \par \uc2 Sluggish growth will also keep price pressures down. The bank said the output gap would remain negative during 2014, which means the economy will not be growing fast enough to add to inflation pressures this year.\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-2-15T21:13:20.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140212ea2c00spg\par }\page {\par\f