\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ Blog:Fed Uncertainty Seen Keeping Bank of Mexico on Hold\b0\par\par\uc2 644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2013\par 18:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Real Time Economics blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/economics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/economics}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Anthony Harrup\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  might be sorely tempted to cut interest rates this week after the economy took a turn for the worse in the second quarter and inflation slowed, but consensus is that the specter of Fed "tapering" and its potential fallout in emerging markets will keep policy makers on hold, settling instead for some very dovish language.\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2 's five-member board met in July, inflation has moved back within its 2%-4% target range and the government statistics agency has reported that gross domestic product contracted 0.7% in the second quarter from the first, prompting a cascade of downward revisions for full-year GDP growth.\par \par \uc2 Local conditions, most economists agree, are ripe for a cut in the overnight rate from the current 4%, but they're also almost unanimous in thinking that the bank will be kept from taking the plunge by the prospects of the U.S. Federal Reserve heading in the other direction with a gradual reduction in its asset-buying program. Already, anticipation of Fed tapering has contributed to a 10% depreciation of the peso since early May as higher U.S. yields take the shine off emerging-market assets.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of the Fed equation, a cutback in stimulus would imply that the U.S. economy is on track for stronger growth, which would fuel the expected pick-up in Mexico's economy.\par \par \uc2 In some ways, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  led by Governor Agust\u237\'00\'EDn Carstens, has it easier than peers elsewhere in the developing world. Brazil, Indonesia and Turkey have all raised interest rates recently as they grapple with weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rising prices. The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to contain volatility in the peso, and a mechanism for auctioning dollars on particularly bad days was suspended in April. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says passthrough from the exchange rate to inflation is low.\par \par \uc2 Government plans to submit a tax overhaul proposal to Congress on Sept. 8 could also stay the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hand, particularly if it seeks to charge tax on currently exempt food and medicines, which would have a one-time effect on consumer prices.\par \par \uc2 That the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will sound dovish in its statement Friday is a given, says Nomura strategist Benito Berber. "How could it not? Even if \uc2 Banxico\uc2  [\uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2 ] only describes the current state of the economy the message will come across as markedly dovish."\par \par \uc2 The question is whether or not the bank will cut rates, or open the door to cuts, and "the only scenario in which we would project rate cuts is if the Fed delays on tapering," he added.\par \par \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  economist Alonso Cervera assigns a 60% chance of the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  lowering the overnight rate by a quarter of a percentage point to 3.75% this week, and 40% chance it will stay on hold, but sees little point in an easing bias if inflation risks are likely to shape up before the next meeting in late October, making it harder to justify rate cuts possibly for months.\par \par \uc2 "While there will always be risks associated with policy decisions, the fragile state of the Mexican economy and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's success in controlling inflation make the risk of cutting the overnight rate worth taking, in our view," he said in a report.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  is scheduled to release its interest rate decision and statement on Friday at 10 a.m. EDT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 04, 2013 12:27 ET (16:27 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130904e99400471\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
