\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: Mexico leads LatAm decline ahead of Fed minutes\b0\par\par\uc2 1,446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2013\par 17:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso leads today's decline among Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , falling 1.3% against the dollar, amid nervousness ahead of today's July FOMC minutes. "On a day like this, you're getting a bigger spike in Mexico," says Italo Lombardi, Latin American economist at Standard Chartered. He notes Mexico is particularly correlated with the US economy and feels any shift in that country's landscape more acutely than other LatAm countries. There's also "basically no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so [the peso] responds more aggressively to spikes in risk aversion," Whereas, declines in Brazil, Colombia and Peru have been somewhat stemmed by \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions in the market. The dollar is up 1.3% at MXN13.1444.(lauren.davidson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:59 EDT - The $114.1B in redemptions from bond mutual funds and ETFs since the start of June "marks an enormous shift" for bonds, says \uc2 David Santschi\uc2 , chief executive of TrimTabs. Before June, bond funds posted inflows for 21 straight months, he says. Many who poured $1.20T into the funds from 2009-2012 probably didn't grasp the interest-rate risk, Santschi says. It suggests that if the Fed starts tapering QE, "it might be more disruptive" than expected, he adds. (daisy.maxey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:44 EDT - \uc2 PetSmart\uc2  (PETM) 2Q results were somewhat in line -- a 3c beat on EPS along with a slight miss on revenue -- and the retailer raised its full-year guidance. Investors aren't impressed, though. PETM, which bought back $24M in stock during 2Q, may have raised its FY EPS view, but same-store sales estimate stayed put at 3%-4%. Not too encouraging after the company just posted its third-straight quarter of weaker SSS growth compared to prior-year periods. PETM down 3.9% to $72.11. (michael.calia@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Price action in emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  today isn't making developing countries' central banks look terribly effective. The Turkish lira continues to decline even after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hiked rates earlier this week and announced an open-ended series of $100M in daily \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While EM units would likely be falling even farther without such steps, the relentless losses in the sector raises the question of whether investors are losing confidence in policy makers' game plans to calm markets. The dollar gets as high as TRY1.9698, its highest since July 8, and is currently at TRY1.9668. (erin.mccarthy@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:34 EDT - There are a couple ways to read mining and commodity trade giant \uc2 Glencore\uc2  Xstrata's bullish copper market outlook (In brief: they say stockpiles in China are down by 2/3 this year, and demand is much better than the market thinks). By its own estimates before the company's 2011 IPO, \uc2 Glencore\uc2  handles about 50% of the world's traded copper, lending it an extraordinary window into the market. On the other hand, talking up the copper price is low-hanging fruit for a company that lives for arbitrage opportunities and is, after its Xstrata purchase, the fourth-largest miner of the metal. (matt.day@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee fell to a fresh record low, leading a decline across the board for emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of this afternoon's Fed minutes, which could shed light on the timeline of QE tapering. The dollar rose more than 2% against the rupee, trading recently at INR 64.38, and climbed 1.7% to the Indonesian rupiah to change hands at IDR10,912. Central banks continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in attempts to shore up their country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but are struggling to stem the flow of investors moving their money back into developed markets. Despite their central banks selling dollars, the Brazilian real fell to BRL2.4137 per dollar and the Turkish lira fell to TRY1.9685 against the dollar. (lauren.davidson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TrimTabs says net outflows from bond mutual funds and ETFs through Monday at $30.9B was already the 3rd-highest level on record, though still less than half of June's $69.1B. But is it more than double of what was pulled all of last month from the instruments, and CEO \uc2 David Santschi\uc2  says what's happened within the past 90 days shows "many investors probably didn't grasp the interest-rate risk they were taking" by plowing into the sector the past 4 years. "Now that they're suffering losses in funds they regarded as 'safe,' they want out fast." The research firm notes that in the past month, outflows have been heaviest in munis and Treasurys. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:12 EDT - US stocks not quite sure what to do yet with a much better-than-expected July existing home sales report. Economists had forecast sales to be up 1.4% but instead they climbed 6.5%, the best month since the days of the homebuyer tax credit in 2009. \uc2 Dow\uc2  Industrials initialed jogged in place, then got a modest lift and now topping and easing back a bit. Stronger housing is, of course, encouraging for the nation's economic prospects, but also adds to the argument that the Fed should ratchet back its easy-money policies, which isn't necessarily good for stocks near term. DJIA down 34; S&P 500 off 3; Nasdaq up 2. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:00 EDT - While admitting downgrading American Eagle (AEO) at this point is "highly reactionary," Piper Jaffray says today's full F2Q report and 3Q guidance suggests "that an improvement in sales pace is not likely despite the broader pickup in retail traffic that we have observed into August" and is likely to continue next month. The investment bank, which goes to neutral on the teen-apparel chain and lowers its price target to $16 from $23, notes, there's "likely to be multiple questions around the company's ability to manage and execute in a highly volatile sales environment--which is the new normal." AEO falls 8.7% to $14.96 as shares hit a 17-month low. Abercrombie (ANF) and Aeropostale (ARO) each drop 1.7%. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The bond market is tilting toward the possibility of tapering signals in this afternoon's Fed minutes. Bond yields march higher and the 10-year yield just touched a session high of 2.855%, moving closer to the 2.899% set on Monday. "I think if [the minutes] read as if September is a foregone conclusion, Treasurys should retest the lows from earlier in the week," says Anthony Cronin, Treasury trader at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . "If they leave any ambiguity as to timing, and/or mention higher rates potentially derailing the recovery, then the market may rally." The 10-year note is recently 9/32 lower, yielding 2.847%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 9:44 EDT - Better late than never. Argus raises \uc2 Herbalife (HLF) \uc2 to buy from hold, based on its expectation for "earnings to remain strong" and "management's comments that the company's balance sheet is underlevered," which could lead to accelerated share buybacks. The call follows HLF rallying 6.9% since July 29, when it reported earnings and its CFO, \uc2 John DeSimone\uc2 , noted the "under-levered" balance sheet. HLF closed yesterday at $64.72. Argus sees it going to $80, so while the analysts may have missed some of the move since the earnings print, plenty of upside remains if they're right. Shares up 1.7% at $65.79. (matthew.jarzemsky@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:26 EDT - \uc2 Tupperware\uc2  (TUP) shares look "fairly priced, BMO says, after rising about 34% this year and almost 58% in the past 12 months, and it downgrades TUP to market perform from outperform. The firm also thinks there's some profit risk from Indonesia, which accounts for about 8% of TUP's sales, and where "the price of gas has increased 44% and the rupiah is down 9% YTD and 4% since TUP reported 2Q earnings on July 24 as the government tries to address the fiscal issues there." Also, BMO says TUP's top seven markets account for roughly 50% of sales, though "sales seem robust only in Brazil and China." (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 08-21-13 1114ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020130821e98l0008i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
