\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: Mexico leads LatAm decline ahead of Fed minutes\b0\par\par\uc2 1,522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2013\par 17:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso leads today's decline among Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , falling 1.3% against the dollar, amid nervousness ahead of today's July FOMC minutes. "On a day like this, you're getting a bigger spike in Mexico," says Italo Lombardi, Latin American economist at Standard Chartered. He notes Mexico is particularly correlated with the US economy and feels any shift in that country's landscape more acutely than other LatAm countries. There's also "basically no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so [the peso] responds more aggressively to spikes in risk aversion," Whereas, declines in Brazil, Colombia and Peru have been somewhat stemmed by \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions in the market. The dollar is up 1.3% at MXN13.1444.(lauren.davidson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Price action in emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  today isn't making developing countries' central banks look terribly effective. The Turkish lira continues to decline even after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hiked rates earlier this week and announced an open-ended series of $100M in daily \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While EM units would likely be falling even farther without such steps, the relentless losses in the sector raises the question of whether investors are losing confidence in policy makers' game plans to calm markets. The dollar gets as high as TRY1.9698, its highest since July 8, and is currently at TRY1.9668. (erin.mccarthy@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee fell to a fresh record low, leading a decline across the board for emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of this afternoon's Fed minutes, which could shed light on the timeline of QE tapering. The dollar rose more than 2% against the rupee, trading recently at INR 64.38, and climbed 1.7% to the Indonesian rupiah to change hands at IDR10,912. Central banks continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in attempts to shore up their country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but are struggling to stem the flow of investors moving their money back into developed markets. Despite their central banks selling dollars, the Brazilian real fell to BRL2.4137 per dollar and the Turkish lira fell to TRY1.9685 against the dollar. (lauren.davidson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Perhaps on any other day, July home sales rising 6.5% (versus 1.4% expectations) would have sent the dollar shooting higher. But alas, traders today have bigger fish to fry--namely the 2:00pm EDT release of minutes from the Fed's last rate-setting meeting, seen as a key bellwether of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's feelings regarding an imminent rollback of its bond-buying program. If the minutes indicate that September tapering of stimulus is still very much on the table, the good home sales data will only add fuel to the fire. For now, dollar is pretty much unchanged against its rivals: USD/JPY at Y97.61; EUR/USD at $1.3386, down 0.2%. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TrimTabs says net outflows from bond mutual funds and ETFs through Monday at $30.9B was already the 3rd-highest level on record, though still less than half of June's $69.1B. But is it more than double of what was pulled all of last month from the instruments, and CEO \uc2 David Santschi\uc2  says what's happened within the past 90 days shows "many investors probably didn't grasp the interest-rate risk they were taking" by plowing into the sector the past 4 years. "Now that they're suffering losses in funds they regarded as 'safe,' they want out fast." The research firm notes that in the past month, outflows have been heaviest in munis and Treasurys. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The bond market is tilting toward the possibility of tapering signals in this afternoon's Fed minutes. Bond yields march higher and the 10-year yield just touched a session high of 2.855%, moving closer to the 2.899% set on Monday. "I think if [the minutes] read as if September is a foregone conclusion, Treasurys should retest the lows from earlier in the week," says Anthony Cronin, Treasury trader at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . "If they leave any ambiguity as to timing, and/or mention higher rates potentially derailing the recovery, then the market may rally." The 10-year note is recently 9/32 lower, yielding 2.847%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 The last set of minutes (for June FOMC meeting) showed the staff brightening its growth outlook as stocks and home prices rose, but also that these positive factors were partially offset by higher interest rates. In the month and a half between the June and July meetings, rates rose further. Karim Basta, chief investment strategist at fixed-income-focused hedge fund III Associates, wants to know to what degree those higher yields will dent the Fed's outlook. As of now, he sees the Fed reducing bond purchases by $10B-20B in September and stopping entirely by mid-2014. He says minutes may also clue to the breakdown of the reduction between Treasurys and MBS if it reveals extra concern about higher mortgage rates hurting housing demand. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 8:53 EDT - While \uc2 Eaton Vance\uc2 's (EV) F3Q results didn't grow quite as much as analysts anticipated, the money manager did generated its 2nd-highest quarter of net inflows at $8.8B despite the bond market's rout the past several months. The company logged sharp growth in its floating-rate income products, more than offsetting modest outflows from equities and fixed income. Floating-rate instruments saw $4.9B of net inflows in 2Q and another $4.4B last quarter as they benefit from the rising long-term-rate environment. The segment's assets under management have soared more than 50% the past year to $38B, making it more than 10% of EV's total. Shares, inactive premarket, have jumped 23% this year. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Bond prices edge lower following yesterday's rally. Investors are cautious ahead of FOMC minutes due at 2:00pm EDT, hoping for some clarity on the Fed's monetary policy path. Expectations for QE tapering as soon as next month have been the key driver pushing bond yields to two-year highs this week. If the minutes cement tapering on its way, bond yields may rise; if taper timing remains uncertain, yields could dip. Another focus is the Fed's debate on forward guidance. Fed officials have signaled that even if they move to taper bond buying, they may strengthen forward guidance to keep policy rate near zero for a long time. July existing home sales due at 10:00am. The 10-year note recently 2/32 lower, yielding 2.823%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 0928 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to keep its policy rate unchanged at 3.00% for the rest of 2013 as inflation remains modest, says AmResearch; "Inflation growth in 2013 will remain modest while upside price pressure remains limited at this juncture," says AmResearch economist Patricia Oh. She notes that inflation increased a modest 1.7% year-to-date, and expects full-year CPI growth to come in at 2.0%. Malaysia's consumer price index, the country's main gauge of inflation, is up 2.0% in July from a year earlier, official data show, matching the median forecast of 15 economists in a \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  poll; CPI is up 0.2% from a month earlier. (jason.ng@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0704 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/KRW falls to 1,117.4 late Wednesday vs 1,120.8 late Tuesday in Seoul, as investors waited to hear more on the future of the U.S. Federal Reserve's easy monetary policy. Traders largely remained cautious about taking any aggressive positions ahead of the release of the July FOMC minutes early Thursday morning Seoul time, says Woori Futures analyst Son Eun-jeong. Analysts tip the USD/KRW in a 1,110-1,125 range for Thursday. (in-soo.nam@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 minutes to be released at 1800 GMT. The rupee weakens Wednesday to 63.41, reversing some of Tuesday's recovery from an all-time low. The USD/INR could range within 63.00-64.00 for now. The dollar is holding firm ahead of the highly anticipated release of the U.S. Federal Reserve's July FOMC minutes, which could hint at a September tapering of QE. The latest move by India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which granted a temporary reprieve to the INR, appears to have had limited effect. The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  late Tuesday announced it would purchase more government bonds to increase rupee liquidity. The change of heart comes as the rupee hit a third consecutive record low of 64.13 to the dollar. The slew of stopgap measures that the \uc2 Indian government\uc2  has introduced this month have been seen as a poor attempt to reduce the chronic current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 USD/INR is now 63.46. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) [ 21-08-13 1514GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130821e98l000i2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
