\par\par \b \uc2 REUTERS SUMMIT-Mexico says U.S. economy a worry for growth\b0\par\par\uc2 862 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2013\par 03:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (For other news from Reuters Latin American Investment Summit, click on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.reuters.com/summit/LatinAmerica13"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.reuters.com/summit/LatinAmerica13}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2  * Unclear if slowdown could spur spending cuts\par \par \uc2  * Government working to keep political pact on key reforms\par \par \uc2  * After huge flows, Mexico preparing for Fed withdrawal \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments, background)\par \par \uc2  By Michael O'Boyle and Krista Hughes\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, May 22 (Reuters) - Mixed signals from the U.S. economy are clouding the growth outlook for Mexico and it needs to be ready for the shocks that could accompany a possible withdrawal of U.S. monetary stimulus, Finance Minister Luis Videgaray said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2  Mexico's government last week cut its growth outlook for 2013 to 3.1 percent from 3.5 percent after a soft first quarter, and Videgaray said Latin America's No. 2 economy has been hurt by weaker U.S. demand for its exports. \par \par \uc2  "Without a doubt the most important thing is the weakness in the growth of the United States ... which still shows mixed signals," Videgaray told the Reuters Latin America Investment Summit in Mexico City. "We have good data in the United States and the next day we have discouraging data."\par \par \uc2  Mexico sends nearly 80 percent of its exports to its northern neighbor and solid U.S. demand for Mexican goods has helped support its economy during sluggish global growth during the last two years.\par \par \uc2  "Clearly, we think that the U.S. economy has a good horizon out there, but we are still not there, and without a doubt this affects Mexican exports and aggregate Mexican demand," he said. \par \par \uc2  Videgaray said it was still unclear if Mexico's government, which has to balance its budget this year by law, would need to cut public spending.\par \par \uc2  "It is still too premature to establish if there will be a drop in income or not. Remember, other elements of public revenue are above what was established in the budget law, such as the price of oil," Videgaray said.\par \par \uc2  Mexico's 2013 budget estimated an average oil price of $86 per barrel, while the price of its export mix has averaged about $94 per barrel so far this year <MEX-OSP>. \par \par \uc2  Videgaray managed President \uc2 Enrique Pena Nieto\uc2 's campaign last year and is seen as one of the architects of a pact with the two main opposition parties to pursue an ambitious reform agenda.\par \par \uc2  Mexico's divided Congress has approved major education and telecommunications bills since Pena Nieto took office in December, appearing to mark a break with about 15 years of legislative deadlock on tough issues.\par \par \uc2  However, divisions within the main conservative opposition party could undermine support for the pact ahead of talks on key energy and fiscal reforms planned for the second half of the year. \par \par \uc2  "The Pact for Mexico is being tested every day and the government is working every day to preserve this climate of cordiality that allows for these agreements," Videgaray said. \par \par \uc2  EYES ON FEDERAL RESERVE\par \par \uc2  He said Mexico is eyeing the possibility that the U.S. Federal Reserve will begin paring back its massive monetary stimulus, which has helped the U.S. economy and fed into global demand for riskier assets such as emerging market debt and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Policymakers in emerging markets around the world are concerned that their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are becoming too strong and that the huge capital inflows could quickly reverse if the Federal Reserve pulls back from the stimulus measures.\par \par \uc2  "This was one of the important themes discussed at the G20 meetings in Washington: what are the exit mechanisms when liquidity returns to normal levels. Without any doubt, Mexico is preparing for it and Mexico has to be prepared," he said.\par \par \uc2  "It is not something that we think will happen in the coming weeks, nor probably in the coming months, but eventually global monetary policy will return to normal," Videgaray said.\par \par \uc2  Foreign holdings of peso-denominated debt have risen more than six-fold since the start of 2009 to about 1.75 trillion Mexican pesos ($142 billion) this May. Mexico has nearly $170 billion in reserves plus a flexible credit line with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  worth more than $70 billion.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its benchmark interest rate in March to a record low of 4.0 percent in what was seen as a bid to try to slow the tide of yield-hungry investors surging into Mexican markets. Analysts expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cut interest rates again once a current spike in inflation subsides.\par \par \uc2  Yields on Mexican peso bonds have hit successive record lows, while Mexico's peso  earlier this month hit its strongest level since August 2011.  \par \par \uc2  Videgaray said there were no current plans to change the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eschewed the types of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that other emerging market economies have used to try to tame unwanted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains.\par \par \uc2  Follow Reuters Summits on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @Reuters_Summits ($1 = 12.3328 Mexican pesos) \par \par \uc2 (Editing by Dave Graham, Kieran Murray and Paul Simao)\par \par \uc2