\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Bank of Mexico Keeps Rates Unchanged\b0\par\par\uc2 778 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2013\par 18:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Policy makers expect the rise in inflation to be temporary\par \par \uc2 --\uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  says the main risk for Mexico would be a U.S. slowdown\par \par \uc2 --Markets are still expecting a rate cut later in the year\par \par \uc2 (Adds details and background throughout.)\par \par \uc2 By Matthew Walter\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY--The \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  voted to leave its benchmark lending rate unchanged this week and provided little indication it will cut rates in the future despite recent signs of slowing economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The bank's policy-setting board voted to hold the overnight rate target at 4% after cutting it by half a percentage point at its March meeting, which had been the first rate adjustment in four years. Economists widely anticipated the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  would leave monetary policy alone at its Thursday meeting, and only one of the 22 banks in a Banamex survey expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease rates again later this year.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  reinforced those expectations in its statement Friday, saying it views the recent rise in the country's annual inflation rate above its target range to be a temporary phenomenon. While acknowledging that Mexico's economy has slowed, policymakers said the biggest risk to economic growth would be a more intense slowdown in the U.S. economy, should it materialize.\par \par \uc2 "External demand and some consumption indicators continue showing signs of weakness," the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  said in its statement. "Inflation expectations in the medium and long term have remained stable."\par \par \uc2 Economic data published earlier this week demonstrate the challenges the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is facing. Annual inflation rose to 4.72% in early April, well above the 4% ceiling of the bank's target range. Meanwhile, the latest growth indicator showed the economy slowed in the first part of the year, with the Mexican statistics agency's economic-activity indicator rising just 0.4% in February, far short of the 1.5% increase economists had forecast.\par \par \uc2 "Policy makers are facing a delicate balancing act," notes David Rees, an emerging markets economist at Capital Economics. "In the near term, high inflation will prevent further rate cuts despite weak economic activity. Beyond that, stronger growth looks set to block additional easing."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov. Agustin Carstens said Thursday during a banking convention in Acapulco that inflation could start to slow again in June, falling towards the 3% target in the second half of the year. But he also warned that market volatility and accelerating foreign investment could provoke an overreaction in the exchange rate that Mexican authorities will have to monitor closely.\par \par \uc2 The peso has appreciated more than 6% against the dollar this year as foreign investors have piled into Mexico's bond market, leading some to speculate that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might decide to take some action to help relieve pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The concern is that a stronger peso might undercut the competitiveness of the country's exports.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  typically avoids surprising investors with direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, preferring instead to schedule auctions to buy and sell dollars. But policy makers didn't send any signal Friday that they are preparing to try to weaken the peso.\par \par \uc2 "Their only real concern is volatility, or that the exchange rate might rise too quickly," said Arnoldo Lopez, an economist at \uc2 BBVA Bancomer\uc2  in Mexico City. "In terms of structural problems, [the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] is not a risk for the economy" at the moment.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's exports declined 1.6% in the first quarter, driven by falling prices for oil exports, the country's statistics agency reported Friday. However, exports of manufactured goods including cars, which are more sensitive to the exchange rate, increased in the quarter, indicating the stronger peso isn't yet causing problems for the industrial sector.\par \par \uc2 The peso traded Friday at 12.1680 to the dollar, little changed from before the policy decision was announced and from Thursday's close at 12.1710.\par \par \uc2 Despite the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2 's neutral stance this week, some market participants continue to expect a rate cut at some point this year. Before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy decision was announced, investment bank Nomura said the market had been pricing in another quarter point cut in the interest rate futures market, and there was little change in the market after the statement was published.\par \par \uc2 "They are going to take their time, but the balance of risks points towards a rate cut," said Marco Oviedo, an economist at \uc2 Barclays\uc2  in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 Write to Matthew Walter at matthew.walter@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 26, 2013 12:45 ET (16:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130426e94q0058m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
