\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Scant room for more rate cuts in Latin America this year\b0\par\par\uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2013\par 18:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * For Latin America poll data click on\par \par \uc2 * GDP forecasts broadly unchanged from Jan poll\par \par \uc2 * Economists see growth divide between Pacific and Atlantic\par \par \uc2 By Silvio Cascione\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, April 11 (Reuters) - Latin American central banks will probably leave interest rates unchanged for now after relatively low inflation allowed them to give their economies an extra boost earlier this year, a Reuters poll showed on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The clear exception is Brazil, where inflation has pierced the official target ceiling and will probably force the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates soon.\par \par \uc2 The poll of 95 economists and analysts worldwide also underscored that economic growth is not at peril in most of Latin America, with forecasts broadly unchanged for most of the countries from a similar poll conducted three months ago.\par \par \uc2 In the latest regional Reuters poll in January, economists presaged correctly that there would be a window of opportunity to trim interest rates after a surprise slowdown in inflation. While prices have not become a threat in most of the region, economists now see scant room for further rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  trimmed its benchmark lending rate to a record low of 4 percent last month. It will probably leave it at that level through end-2014, according to the median of 18 forecasts in the poll.\par \par \uc2 Colombian policymakers took more aggressive action and chopped interest rates to 3.25 percent, the lowest in the region, as inflation hovered below the bottom end of the inflation target band. Now they will probably leave rates unchanged for the rest of the year, hiking them back to 4.25 percent only in 2014, the poll showed.\par \par \uc2 The possibility of further easing should not be ruled out, though, as a tool to fight massive capital inflows triggered by easy monetary policies in countries like Japan.\par \par \uc2 "If the Mexican peso continues to appreciate fast, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might consider further rate cuts," wrote \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's economists led by \uc2 Sergio Martin\uc2  in a research note.\par \par \uc2 In Chile, authorities have considered raising rates to prevent the economy from overheating.\par \par \uc2 Economists say no action will be taken until the fourth quarter of this year as forecasts for inflation remain comfortably within the government's target.\par \par \uc2 Concerns about excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains should also affect monetary policy in Peru, where borrowing costs should be unchanged at 4.25 percent through the end of 2014, at least.\par \par \uc2 GROWTH DIVIDE\par \par \uc2 Despite an overall healthy outlook for economic growth in Latin America, economists notice a gap between fast-growing Pacific countries like Peru and Chile, and slower Atlantic economies such as Brazil and Argentina - where heavy government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has scared off foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to outperform Brazil over the next few years. And while near-term growth in Peru and Chile should hold up fairly well, we expect Venezuela and Argentina to flirt with recession for the foreseeable future," wrote economists at Capital Economics in a note.\par \par \uc2 Mexico and Colombia, where interest rates dropped recently, will both grow between 3.5 and 4.5 percent in 2013 and 2014, and Peru will continue to expand by more than 6 percent annually - one of the best performances in the world.\par \par \uc2 Among the Latin American countries with inflation-targeting regimes, Brazil is the clear exception.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which shocked markets by slashing interest rates 10 straight times in the past two years, is expected to raise borrowing costs from May on to push inflation back to the target bank.\par \par \uc2 The poll also showed that the political uncertainty in Venezuela after the death of President \uc2 Hugo Chavez\uc2  has taken a toll on forecasts for economic growth. The oil exporter will probably grow just 1.0 percent this year, down from a previous estimate of 2.0 percent, the median of 11 forecasts showed.\par \par \uc2 (For other stories from the poll, see: )\par \par \uc2 (Additional polling by Latin America Reuters bureaux, Reuters Polls Bangalore team; Editing by Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-LATAM/POLL | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | U | C | O | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130414e94b0008w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
