\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  weakens on Cyprus; real breaks 2/dlr mark\b0\par\par\uc2 551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 20:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * EU gives Cyprus ultimatum to raise money or face bankruptcy\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real crosses 2/dlr mark for 1st time since Jan 30\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  says any \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be rules-based\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real drops 0.6 pct, Mexico peso 0.5 pct lower\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, March 21 (Reuters) - The Brazilian real and the Mexican peso slid on Thursday as fears of a financial meltdown in Cyprus, combined with signs of economic weakness in Europe, drove investors to the safety of the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real dropped 0.6 percent, crossing the 2-per-dollar mark that many analysts considered a boundary of an informal trading band imposed by policymakers.\par \par \uc2 It was the first time since Jan. 30 that the real traded weaker than 2 per dollar, leaving investors on guard for a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 "We have to see if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will act now that the real has weakened past 2 per dollar," said Jaime Ferreira, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with Intercam brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 "I believe it is too early for an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but, if this lasts till Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may very well do something."\par \par \uc2 Analysts believe the \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants the real to remain within 1.95-2.0 per dollar, slightly stronger than it was at the end of 2012, to help lower the price of imported goods and anchor inflation expectations.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso lost 0.5 percent as investors feared an escalation of the euro zone crisis if Cyprus' banking system is allowed to collapse in the next few days.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the \uc2 European Union\uc2  gave the island an ultimatum to raise the billions of euros it needs to secure an international bailout before the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cuts off liquidity to Cypriot banks.\par \par \uc2 "People are worried about Cyprus. Either it accepts the EU conditions or it goes bankrupt," said Reginaldo Galhardo, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager with Treviso brokerage.\par \par \uc2 Investor concerns were also fueled by purchasing managers' data showing French businesses had their worst performance in four years in March, likely pushing the euro zone's second-largest economy into recession, while Germany also seemed to be running out of steam.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had hit its strongest level in more than 1-1/2 years on Wednesday, leaving some investors wondering whether the government was growing uncomfortable with the strength of the peso.\par \par \uc2 But on Thursday Finance Minister Luis Videgaray said Mexico will continue with its flexible exchange rate and that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market would be based on rules.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 1850 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                          daily %    YTD %\par                                   change   change\par                          Latest           \par Brazil real                2.0010    -0.60     1.95\par                                           \par Mexico peso               12.4083    -0.50     3.67\par                                           \par Chile peso               472.6000     0.06     1.29\par                                           \par Colombia peso           1819.8200    -0.09    -2.96\par                                           \par Peru sol                   2.5910     0.00    -1.54\par                                           \par Argentina peso             5.0975     0.00    -3.63\par (interbank)                                 \par                                           \par Argentina peso             8.4000     4.17   -19.29\par (parallel)                                  \par                                           \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Natalia Cacioli and Bruno Federowski in Sao Paulo; editing by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jim Marshall\f28 \uc2 \f2 )\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  | ABN | D | M | RBN | NAT | C | E | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130324e93l000aa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
