\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  finmin- any  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  would be rules-based\b0\par\par\uc2 470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 18:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Adds detail, background)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, March 21 (Reuters) - Mexican Finance Minister Luis Videgaray on Thursday said his country will continue with its flexible exchange rate and that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be rules-based.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso hit its strongest levels in more than 1-1/2 years this week, but Latin America's No. 2 economy has avoided using the direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and capital controls that other emerging-market countries have used to tame \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains.\par \par \uc2  The peso's strong gains have fueled speculation that Mexico could reinstate a rules-based mechanism of auctioning dollar "put" options, which could show authorities are growing uncomfortable with the pace of peso gains. \par \par \uc2  "We will continue with a free-float exchange rate policy," Videgaray said at an event hosted by U.S. news agency Bloomberg in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2  Deputy Finance Minister Fernando Aportela told Reuters on Saturday that Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission, made up of members of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the finance ministry, had not discussed any new measures to try to slow the peso's advance.\par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  committee's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be as it has been until now: with rules that are made known previously and that aim to moderate abrupt variations," Videgaray said in response to a question.\par \par \uc2  Mexico's peso has surged more than 3 percent this month, backed by growing optimism about the government's ability to push long-stalled economic reforms through a divided Congress.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its benchmark interest rate to a record low of 4 percent on March 8 in what market players read as a bid to curb the appeal of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to yield-hungry investors.\par \par \uc2  While lower rates could deter some speculators, analysts expect the peso to keep gaining ground due to euphoria about the chance for reforms and potential credit-ratings upgrades. \par \par \uc2  The peso has yet to recover to the levels seen before late 2008, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's relative cheapness has boosted Mexican exports amid sluggish global growth.\par \par \uc2  The \uc2 Banco de Mexico\uc2  warned on March 8 that heavy inflows could lead to an 'unnecessary' tightening in monetary conditions. A stronger peso could create drag on growth by crimping exporters' profits.\par \par \uc2  Economy Minister Ildefonso Guarjardo said at the same event on Thursday that any loss to Mexico's competitiveness would be offset by gains from the series of planned economic reforms, including key energy and tax bills due later this year. \par \par \uc2  Mexican lawmakers were set to vote on Thursday on a  sweeping telecommunications bill that would threaten dominant companies with tougher regulation.  \par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Krista Hughes and Luis Rojas; Editing by James Dalgleish)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO-ECONOMY/PESO (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | U | C | O | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130321e93l00127\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
