\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Mexico peso rise tests  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2 's hands-off stance\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jean Arce and Krista Hughes  \par \uc2 940 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 12:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, March 14 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso is scaling new highs amid confidence in the country's reform push, a likely credit ratings upgrade and profit taking after last week's interest rate cut, sparking speculation about how far it will rise before authorities act.\par \par \uc2 The peso has gained 2.6 percent since the start of the month, the best performance of the 152 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked by Thomson Reuters, and broken through key levels of 12.50 per dollar for the first time in 18 months.\par \par \uc2 The rise has come despite a 50 basis point cut in benchmark interest rates to a record low 4 percent on Friday, which only served to fuel investor optimism about the outlook for Latin America's No. 2 economy.\par \par \uc2 A signal from ratings agency Standard and Poor's that it might upgrade Mexico's credit rating and the announcement of plans to introduce more competition into the communications sector have also boosted Mexico's standing.\par \par \uc2 "As an investor, this is positive ... and will attract more capital to Mexico," said Rudy Amoresano, founder of boutique fund manager Caerus Management, of the reform plan.\par \par \uc2 More capital is the last thing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants. The \uc2 Banco de Mexico\uc2  warned that heavy inflows could lead to an 'unnecessary' tightening in monetary conditions, as a stronger peso would drag on growth.\par \par \uc2 "The market is testing to see how far the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will allow the peso to appreciate without taking any kind of action,"\par \par \uc2 said Enrique Alvarez, Latin America strategist at IDEAglobal.\par \par \uc2 Even though the rate cut has not dissuaded investors in the short term, the famously non-\b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2 Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has more options up its sleeve.\par \par \uc2 It could officially drop a mechanism triggering an automatic offer of dollars if the peso weakens sharply, introduced when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was going through a weak patch in late 2011.\par \par \uc2 Banorte-Ixe economist Gabriel Casillas said this could be a first step, possibly followed by reinstating a monthly auction of dollar "put" options. This would build up Mexico's foreign reserves cushion and also pour pesos into the market, helping to curb gains. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did this in 2010 and 2011.\par \par \uc2 "It's an ingenious thing where actually the market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not," said CIBC World Markets Latin America strategist John Welch.\par \par \uc2 Under the program, the \uc2 Banco de Mexico\uc2  auctions the right to sell dollars back to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If the peso appreciates strongly, a bank may exercise its option and it will get more pesos for its dollars than it would on the floating peso market.\par \par \uc2 SPECULATION COOLS\par \par \uc2 Welch said some of the recent peso gains could also be explained by an unwinding of hedge positions put on by investors during a recent rally in Mexican bonds before the rate cut.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of people hedge their rate exposure and their bond exposure by shorting the peso and buying dollars, so once they got the fact of the cut, many of them took profits and they would unwind the hedge, which is they would sell dollar-MX instead of buying it," he said.\par \par \uc2 One welcome sign for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has publicly sworn off any involvement in the '\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars' of competitive devaluations, is that speculators are backing off from record bets on further peso appreciation.\par \par \uc2 Speculative net long positions are down more than 35 percent from mid-January, when the peso reached a 10-month high and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  first warned of lower rates ahead, according to data from the \uc2 U.S. Commodity Futures Trading Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Net bets on a stronger peso were the weakest in three months for the week of March 5. The current week's data, due for release on Friday, will show if that trend has accelerated.\par \par \uc2 But Bank of America Latin America strategist Ezequiel Aguirre said the reduction in the positions was a signal for some traders to get back in.\par \par \uc2 "The position was cleaned out, so now you have a lot of investors getting together to push the peso," he said.\par \par \uc2 Another way to dampen upward pressure on the peso would be to give incentives to accelerate capital outflows, perhaps via Mexican pension funds, or Afores, which hold 1.9 trillion pesos ($153.51 billion) in assets.\par \par \uc2 Peru has raised the foreign investment limit for its pension funds to help stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, although Mexico's funds do not even hold the maximum in offshore assets allowed.\par \par \uc2 Data from regulator CONSAR show the funds hold 15.7 percent of their assets offshore, below the 20 percent limit, but up from the 11.9 percent they invested two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  economist \uc2 Marco Oviedo\uc2  said one reason the peso was not already at the 10.5 per dollar levels seen before the financial crisis was private capital outflows by Mexican residents, including the Afores.\par \par \uc2 Although foreigners pumped a record $80 billion into Mexican stocks and bonds last year, Mexicans sent $7 billion out of the country to invest in markets overseas, and another $26 billion in direct investments - double the level of 2011.\par \par \uc2 But if Mexico gains momentum on reforms, passing the telco changes and pushing ahead with proposals to shake up the tax system and energy sector, foreign capital inflows could push the peso to 12.20 by October, Oviedo said. ($1 = 12.3982 Mexican pesos)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Alexandra Alper; Editing by Eric Walsh)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO-ECONOMY/PESO (ANALYSIS) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | C | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130317e93e00021\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
