\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-EMERGING MARKETS-Mexico peso up 5th day, U.S. data buoys Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 16:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story for additional readers with no changes to headline or text)\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real hovers near unchanged on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso gains 0.4 pct, Brazil real up 0.1 pct\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, March 13 (Reuters) - The Mexican peso gained for a fifth consecutive session on Wednesday, leading Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher, as data showed U.S. retail sales rose in February more than twice as much as economists expected.\par \par \uc2 The data boosted prospects for Mexico's economy, adding to investor optimism about a series of structural reforms planned by the new government of President Enrique Pena Nieto. The United States is the main destination for Mexican exports.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso firmed 0.4 percent to 12.3955 per dollar, piercing the mark of 12.4 per greenback for the first time in 18 months. Since March 6, the peso has gained more than 3 percent against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Pena Nieto, who took office in December, unveiled a plan this week to allow more competition in the telecommunications sector, raising hopes that structural reforms are gaining traction in Latin America's second-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 He has also promised changes to the country's tax system and its state-run energy sector in a bid to attract more investment and boost growth to 6 percent a year, about three times the average of the past decade.\par \par \uc2 The reform outlook encouraged rating agency \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  to signal on Tuesday it may upgrade Mexico's credit rating within the next 18 months.\par \par \uc2 Other Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted more modest gains as an increase in Italy's borrowing costs kept investor optimism in check.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real rose 0.1 percent to 1.9595 per dollar. Its gains were also curbed by expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to curb any sharp moves in the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. data came in better than expected and that is offsetting Europe," said Mauricio Nakahodo, an economic consultant with Tokyo-Mitsubishi bank in Sao Paulo, referring to fears Italy could rekindle the European debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 1445 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                          daily %    YTD %\par                                   change   change\par                          Latest           \par Brazil real                1.9595     0.15     4.11\par                                           \par Mexico peso               12.3955     0.40     3.78\par                                           \par Chile peso               471.0000    -0.04     1.63\par                                           \par Colombia peso           1799.4000     0.04    -1.86\par                                           \par Peru sol                   2.5930     0.04    -1.62\par                                           \par Argentina peso             5.0775     0.00    -3.25\par (interbank)                                 \par                                           \par Argentina peso             7.8500    -0.13   -13.63\par (parallel)                                  \par                                           \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Natalia Cacioli in Sao Paulo; Editing by Jeffrey Benkoe)\