\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  mixed; Mexico peso rallies on reform hopes\b0\par\par\uc2 593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 19:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Investors grow optimistic on Mexico economy, reforms\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real weakens for 2nd day after Monday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  peso gains 0.5 pct, Brazil real drops 0.4 pct\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, March 12 (Reuters) - The Mexican peso hit a fresh 18-month high on Tuesday as investors grew optimistic that structural reforms are gaining traction in the country, further boosting its economic prospects.\par \par \uc2 Other Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted more modest gains, supported by rising metal prices, but the Brazilian real slipped for a second day following a Monday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that halted its appreciation trend.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gained 0.5 percent to 12.4770 per dollar, back to levels not seen since September 2011, after President Enrique Pena Nieto unveiled last night a bill to shake up the country's telecom sector, raising bets that other reforms will follow.\par \par \uc2 "Mexico's structural reforms continue to build momentum. This time it was the Initiative for the Telecom reform signed by Pena Nieto," Citi analysts wrote in a note to clients. "We keep thinking that the Mexican peso will keep on outperforming the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market."\par \par \uc2 The peso has strengthened over 2 percent since Friday, when policymakers announced a one-off cut in the country's base interest rate, which fell to an all-time low of 4.0 percent from 4.5 percent. Rather than reducing the appeal of domestic assets, the decision was seen by investors as further boosting Mexico's economic prospects.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, on the other hand, the real weakened 0.4 percent to 1.9632 per dollar, one day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker than 1.95 per dollar - a mark many analysts consider as a boundary of an informal trading band imposed by policymakers.\par \par \uc2 "In a certain way the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  indicated it is working with a 1.95 real floor to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We are likely to see the exchange rate hovering between 1.95 and 2.0 units per dollar," said Luciano Rostagno, chief strategist with \uc2 WestLB\uc2  bank in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 The real had pierced the 1.95-per-dollar level for the first time in 10 months on Friday, after higher-than-expected inflation data increased bets that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would soon raise interest rates, adding to the appeal of real-denominated assets.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real has gained nearly 4 percent so far this year on bets the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would use the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a tool to cheapen the cost of imported goods and help anchor inflation expectations.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's consumer inflation reached 6.31 percent in the 12-month period through February, nearing the ceiling of a government target of 6.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 In Chile, the peso climbed 0.15 percent to 470.80 per dollar, supported by gains in the price of copper, the country's main export product.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 1820 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                            Daily  YTD