\par\par \b \uc2 PREVIEW-Mexico may cut interest rates for first time since crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 769 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 22:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Policymakers may cut borrowing costs to record low\par \par \uc2 * Inflation uptick could postpone decision to cut\par \par \uc2 * Worries about foreign flows could drive big move\par \par \uc2 * Decision due Friday 0900 local (1500 GMT\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, March 7 (Reuters) - \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may brush off a recent uptick in consumer prices and make its boldest move in four years by cutting borrowing costs on Friday as policymakers claim they are winning the battle against inflation.\par \par \uc2 Sixteen of 21 analysts polled last week expect the \uc2 Banco de Mexico\uc2  to hold its benchmark interest rate at 4.50 percent on Friday, but the other five are calling for a cut of at least 50 basis points to a record low of 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Since January, policymakers have been arguing that prudent monetary policy in the last decade has helped drag inflation steadily down toward the bank's 3 percent target, making room for a one-off adjustment to interest rates.\par \par \uc2 "This is the time to cut. Inflation behavior has been great," said Gabriel Casillas, an economist at Banorte-IXE who expects a 50 basis point to 75 basis point cut on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has clung to orthodox inflation-fighting policies since a devastating financial crisis in the 1990s. The bank now seems set on cashing in on that discipline to lock in lower borrowing costs for the economy and bring Mexican rates in line with easy money policies around the world.\par \par \uc2 While policymakers are loath to discuss the exchange rate as a major factor in their decisions, cutting the interest rate could slow big inflows and keep the peso from becoming too expensive.\par \par \uc2 Strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  can hurt local manufacturers by making imports cheaper. \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a proponent of free markets, has eschewed the direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and capital controls used by other emerging markets such as Brazil to try to deter flows.\par \par \uc2 Still, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Agustin Carstens\uc2  last month warned that lower interest rates were not a "done deal" for Latin America's second-largest economy. In January, the bank's board said a recent drop in inflation from a 2-1/2-year high in September would need to be "consolidated" before cutting rates.\par \par \uc2 Inflation accelerated in February to 3.55 percent and Carstens warned it will tick higher through April - a hump which could keep policymakers from cutting now.\par \par \uc2 "The bank is in no hurry to implement the cut," said \uc2 Marco Oviedo\uc2 , an analyst at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 However, the prospect of inflation back above 4 percent by the next meeting in April may leave policymakers thinking they have a narrow window to act. "If they do not do it now, inflation could become a problem again," said Salvador Orozco, a strategist at Santander in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 Investors are betting on slightly less than even odds for a 25 basis point cut on Friday and are tilting toward 50 basis points in April or June, in line with the majority in the Reuters poll.\par \par \uc2 The last time \uc2 Banco de Mexico\uc2  (\uc2 Banxico\uc2 ) slashed borrowing costs was in the first half of 2009 as the country slumped into a deep recession. The bank cut rates from 8.25 percent to 4.5 percent by July, leaving the main rate lower than the inflation rate at the time.\par \par \uc2 A decline in inflation since then means policy is tighter now than it was in mid-2009. Policymakers could also argue that growth risks justify easier credit after a sharp drop in exports and retail sales at the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's growth is seen slowing this year to 3.5 percent expansion from a 3.9 percent expansion in 2012.\par \par \uc2 ONE LONELY HAWK\par \par \uc2 Carstens has been seen as aching to lower interest rates since his appointment in 2010, and his view may be aided by a new board member.\par \par \uc2 Jose Sidaoui, one of the board members most worried about inflation, was replaced by Javier Guzman, a Yale-trained economist who is seen as favoring loose policy. That leaves Manuel Sanchez as the only inflation "hawk."\par \par \uc2 The last two times the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaned toward lower rates, in 2010 and 2012, global market turmoil hammered the peso and threatened to fan inflation through higher import prices, and the bank backtracked.\par \par \uc2 Now, the peso is trading stronger than the 13-per-dollar level that previously stayed policymakers' hands.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Krista Hughes; Editing by Diane Craft)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO-ECONOMY/RATES (PREVIEW) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | U | C | O | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130310e937000d4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
