\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil yields rise on rate hike bets, real slips\b0\par\par\uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Februar 2013\par 16:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Mantega says rates, not \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , are right tool to fight inflation\par \par \uc2 * Mexico yields flat after investors tame rate cut bets\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real weakens 0.38 pct as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Adds comments, updates prices)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 MEXICO\b0 \highlight\uc2  CITY, Feb 15 (Reuters) - Yields on Brazil's interest-rate futures soared on Friday after the country's finance minister suggested policymakers could raise interest rates to fight rising inflation.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meanwhile, weakened after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop it from strengthening past 1.95 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 In Moscow for a meeting of the G20 group, Brazil's Finance Minister Guido Mantega said that a rise in inflation above the government's target raises a yellow flag and that monetary policy, not the exchange rate, is the right tool to control prices.\par \par \uc2 Nomura analyst Tony Volpon said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to tweak its language in its March decision and remove a pledge to keep its benchmark interest rate at a record low of 7.50 percent for a "prolonged period."\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has put its finger on the trigger," Volpon wrote in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 Yields on interest-rate contracts jumped to price in a strong chance that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lift the base Selic rate as soon as in May.\par \par \uc2 Interest-rate contracts maturing in January 2014, among the most traded, jumped about 21 basis points to bid at 7.62 percent, closing at their highest level in four months.\par \par \uc2 Concerns about inflation have driven the real to strengthen 4 percent so far this year as investors bet policymakers would favor a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cheapen the price of imported goods, but would still be forced to raise the Selic later.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian policymakers have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in recent years, acting to weaken the real against the dollar when they are concerned the exchange rate is hurting local manufacturers, but also moving to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when inflation becomes a worry.\par \par \uc2 The real briefly strengthened past the level of 1.95 per dollar for the first time in nine months early in the session. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  with an auction of reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps - derivative contracts that are designed to weaken the real against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The real last bid down 0.38 percent at 1.9645 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, a source on President Dilma Rousseff's economic team said policymakers are more worried about curbing volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, indicating the real could strengthen past the level of 1.95 per dollar as long as the appreciation is gradual.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Mexican interest rate swaps and bonds posted little change after a volatile week as the market gauges when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Latin America's second biggest economy could cut its 4.50 percent benchmark rate.\par \par \uc2 The interest rate swap market trimmed bets on the chance of a 25 basis point rate cut in March this week after \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Agustin Carstens\uc2  on Wednesday warned that lower interest rates are not a "done deal."\par \par \uc2 Investors have priced in a 25 bps cut by April and some economists are eyeing the chance for a 50 bps or 75 bps point cut this year.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso traded little changed, edging 0.04 percent stronger to 12.6845 per dollar. Mexico's peso has pulled back from a 10-month high hit in January after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signaled it could cut interest rates if inflation keeps cooling.\par \par \uc2 Lower interest rates could curb the attraction of the peso to global investors.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 2220 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                          daily %    YTD %\par                                   change   change\par                          Latest           \par Brazil real                1.9645    -0.38     4.00\par                                           \par Mexico peso               12.6845     0.04     1.41\par                                           \par Chile peso               471.0000    -0.06     1.63\par                                           \par Colombia peso           1787.2300    -0.24    -1.19\par                                           \par Peru sol                   2.5740    -0.27    -0.89\par                                           \par Argentina peso             5.0100    -0.10    -1.95\par (interbank)                                 \par                                           \par Argentina peso             7.6900    -0.39   -11.83\par (parallel)                                  \par                                           \par                                                  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Michael O'Boyle; editing by Andrew Hay)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \hig