\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Mexican peso seen advancing in 2013 on reform hopes\b0\par\par\uc2 577 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2013\par 16:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * For poll data see\par \par \uc2 * Peso seen gaining in 2013 on economic reform hopes\par \par \uc2 * Possible interest rate cut could limit gains in short term\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Feb 6 (Reuters) - The Mexican peso is seen advancing in 2013, backed by expectations that the new government will push through key economic reforms this year, but the risk of an interest rate cut could limit gains in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 The peso is seen firming about 1 percent from Tuesday's close to 12.50 per dollar by next January, according to the median of 15 analysts surveyed by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Investors are optimistic that new President Enrique Pena Nieto will be able to win support in a divided Congress to approve energy reform that will allow greater private investment in the state-run industry.\par \par \uc2 Investors are also expecting progress on a long-stalled tax reform this year.\par \par \uc2 "The political environment has a better outlook," said \uc2 Marco Oviedo\uc2 , an analyst at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in Mexico City who expects the peso to firm to 12.20 per dollar in 12 months.\par \par \uc2 "The president has political capital that he can take advantage of," Oviedo said, adding that progress on energy and fiscal reforms could spur credit ratings agencies to upgrade Mexico's sovereign debt.\par \par \uc2 Solid U.S. demand for Mexican exports is also expected to keep supporting the peso. Mexico's economy grew about 4 percent in 2012, compared to 2011, and it is seen slowing to a 3.5 percent rate in 2013, according to a Reuters poll.\par \par \uc2 RATE CUT EYED\par \par \uc2 Analysts said gains in the Mexican peso could be limited in the short-term due to expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may cut its benchmark interest rate, currently at 4.50 percent, in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 By April, the median target of the Reuters poll puts the peso little changed at 12.61.\par \par \uc2 Last month, \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised analysts and the market by hinting it might lower borrowing costs if inflation continues to cool.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at Banorte-IXE are expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut its benchmark rate by 50 basis points to 75 basis points at its March meeting. The interest rate swaps market is pricing in at least a 25 basis point cut by June.\par \par \uc2 "Even if this will not reduce the flows coming to our country, since it still looks attractive, on the margin it should be a factor that will moderate them," said Juan Alderete, a strategist at Banorte-IXE in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 Foreign holdings of peso-denominated debt surged more than 60 percent in 2012 to 1.571 trillion Mexican pesos ($124.42 billion). Some analysts think Mexican policymakers could be seeking to use the threat of an interest rate cut to curb inflows from yield-hungry investors.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eschewed the ad hoc market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and capital controls used by other emerging markets to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from gaining too much and hurting exporters.\par \par \uc2 But Mexican policymakers are clearly worried that further flows could eventually become dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor \uc2 Agustin Carstens\uc2  said on Tuesday warned that emerging markets may face an exodus of capital when major economies begin to reverse accommodative monetary policy. ($1 = 12.6261 Mexican pesos)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Jean Luis Arce; Editing by Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /MEXICO-POLL | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | U | C | O | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130209e92600072\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
