\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Mexico yields see-saw ahead of cenbank minutes\b0\par\par\uc2 711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2013\par 00:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Mexico minutes could reinforce views on rate cut possibility\par \par \uc2  * Peru, Costa Rica move to limit hot money flows \par \par \uc2  By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2  MEXICO CITY, Jan 30 (Reuters) - Mexican yields were little changed on Thursday ahead of the release of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  minutes that could either deepen or undermine bets that policymakers could soon cut interest rates.\par \par \uc2  Mexican bond yields recently hit record lows, and interest rate swaps are pricing in a 25 basis point cut to the country's benchmark rate of 4.50 percent by September.\par \par \uc2  In a policy U-turn at its January meeting, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved from warning of a potential rate hike to saying it could cut borrowing costs if inflation keeps cooling. Minutes from the meeting are due Friday at 0900 local time.\par \par \uc2  The minutes "may show that there was no consensus, at least one director will highlight that it is not yet the time to (cut) and that we need these trends to consolidate," said Alberto Ramos, an economist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2  One reason to cut might be to discourage flows of hot money into the economy this year that could strengthen the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the point where it makes exports less competitive and crimps growth.\par \par \uc2  The yield on Mexico's benchmark 10-year bond  fell 1 basis point to 5.03 percent after bidding up earlier in the session. The yield is trading just above record lows below 5 percent.\par \par \uc2  The Mexican peso firmed 0.13 percent to 12.7105 per dollar. The peso has shed 1.2 percent since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signaled it could cut rates. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors have been piling into Mexican bonds;  foreign holdings of peso debt jumped 60 percent last year and are still rising. \par \par \uc2  "There is a element of preemptiveness to get ahead of the curve, with the steady flows into bonds," Ramos said. "They do not have an exchange rate problem yet, but they could have one down the road."\par \par \uc2   An easing in the euro zone's debt crisis and new economic stimulus measures in Japan are seen fueling bumper investment flows into Latin America and other emerging markets in 2013 and policymakers are trying to ward off unwanted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eschewed the types of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and capital controls that other countries have used.\par \par \uc2  Peru's sol \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  finished bidding 0.31 percent weaker at 2.575 per dollar on Thursday, a day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightened reserve requirements to soften the impact of heavy capital inflows. \par \par \uc2  The sol is near a 16-year high and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market every day since late August to buy dollars, but it did not step in on Thursday.\par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Costa Rica said it will limit lending to limit the attractiveness of an interest rate gap that has sparked a credit boom and drawn in potentially destabilizing foreign capital. \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian real was little changed as the market eyed how far policymakers would let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firm.\par \par \uc2  The real hit a seven-month high on Tuesday, breaking past the 2-per-dollar mark on bets the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will allow further gains as policymakers fret about a recent move higher in inflation.\par \par \uc2  But its advance has stalled this week after Finance Minister Guido Mantega warned that the government was ready to correct any excessive moves in the exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  Brazil has used a series of measures to keep the real from gaining too much in recent years. The real  closed at 1.9885 per dollar, 0.05 percent weaker than Wednesday's close. \par \par \uc2  Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 21:15 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                          daily %    YTD %\par                                   change   change\par                          Latest           \par Brazil real                1.9885    -0.05     2.59\par                                           \par Mexico peso               12.7105     0.15     1.21\par                                           \par Chile peso               471.1000     0.00     1.61\par                                           \par Colombia peso           1775.2700     0.07    -0.52\par                                           \par Peru sol                   2.5750    -0.31    -0.93\par                                           \par Argentina peso             4.9775    -0.05    -1.31\par (interbank)                                 \par                                           \par Argentina peso             7.8900    -1.14   -14.07\par (parallel)                                  \par                                           \par                                                  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ($1 = 12.7384 Mexican pesos)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Editing by Leslie Adler)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | C | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020