\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Mexico peso firms, Brazil real wobbles near low\b0\par\par\uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2012\par 18:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso gains on optimism about U.S. talks\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real near 3-1/2-year low, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  eyed\par \par \uc2 * Chile peso firms, helped by interest rate hike bets\par \par \uc2 (Updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Nov 20 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso firmed on Tuesday, helped by optimism over U.S. fiscal negotiations, while Brazil's real teetered near a 3-1/2-year low, awaiting signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso, one of the most freely traded emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has whipsawed in tandem with U.S. stocks over worries whether U.S. lawmakers will be able to stave off impending tax hikes and spending cuts that could hobble the economy.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso firmed 0.29 percent to 13.0175 per dollar, its fourth session of gains.\par \par \uc2 The peso has rallied 2 percent back from a 10-week low last week, on hopes the U.S. would avoid the "fiscal cliff," a combination of spending cuts and tax rises due to be implemented under existing law in early 2013 that may cut the federal budget deficit but also tip the economy back into recession.\par \par \uc2 Following U.S. President Barack Obama's re-election, the peso slumped and its technical outlook deteriorated sharply, suggesting deeper losses ahead.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rally during the past four sessions drove the cost of dollars in pesos below its 200-day and 100 day moving averages.\par \par \uc2 After its recent rally, Nomura strategist Benito Berber reckons the peso is currently pricing in about a 10 percent to 15 percent chance of failure by U.S. lawmakers, down from about a 20 to 25 percent chance last week.\par \par \uc2 "There is at least a more amicable tone in the talks, but this is very short term," Berber said, adding the peso could slump back toward 13.50 per dollar if the market starts to bet on failure in Washington.\par \par \uc2 As the peso weakened, total bets on the Chicago exchange for\par \par \uc2 a stronger peso fell to $3.7 billion last week from a record high of $5.5 billion in late September.\par \par \uc2 That still leaves a lot of investors betting on a strong peso, investors who could face stop loss levels above the peso's low last week of 13.29 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real traded nearly flat at 2.0805 per dollar, just off its weakest levels since May 2009 that it hit on Friday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading was low with the local stock market closed for a holiday.\par \par \uc2 After trading in a narrow range since July, the real last week saw its steepest weekly slump since early June. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tumble could push \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso firmed 0.4 percent, following a 1 percent surge on Monday, after strong economic data in the third quarter stoked bets that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could raise interest rates. Higher interest rates attract yield-hungry investors.\par \par \uc2 Chile's economy kept expanding at a fast clip in the third quarter versus the year-earlier period, fueled by a jump in domestic demand, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 