\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil real suffers largest loss in 2 months\b0\par\par\uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2012\par 18:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Risk aversion up on concern about delay in Greek aid\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso trims early losses\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real drops 0.9 pct, Mexican peso down 0.1 pct\par \par \uc2 By Danielle Fonseca\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The Brazilian real on Tuesday posted its largest single-day loss in nearly two months on speculation the government would favor a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the economy, while a rebound in U.S. retail stocks supported the Mexican peso.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general were pressured by concerns about the so-called U.S. fiscal cliff, as well as growing fears that Greece would not receive international aid in time to avert bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 The real closed 0.9 percent lower at 2.0685 per dollar, a four-month low, on market chatter that the government would like to see a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost industrial activity.\par \par \uc2 "There is some speculation, some market rumors that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would tolerate a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker than 2.1 per dollar," said Flavio Serrano, chief economist at BES Investimentos in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real has been stuck within an informal trading band of 2.0 to 2.1 per dollar since early July due to a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on both ends of that range.\par \par \uc2 On different occasions, Brazilian policymakers have suggested that such a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  range would be appropriate to support the domestic industry without stoking inflation, but a number of recent economic indicators have showed the economy is taking longer than expected to recover.\par \par \uc2 FISCAL CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 Also weighing on Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were concerns about the looming U.S. fiscal cliff - a combination of government spending cuts and tax increases scheduled to star in January that could plunge the world's largest economy into another recession.\par \par \uc2 The Chilean peso lost 0.68 percent to 483.60 per dollar, its weakest level in almost three months.\par \par \uc2 "What we have is a very discouraging external scenario," said Mario Battistel, a manager at the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk of Fair brokerage in Sao Paulo, citing the fiscal cliff fears in the United States and that concerns that Greece's problems are hurting all of Europe.\par \par \uc2 Fears that the euro zone's debt crisis could flare up again increased as Greece's international lenders clashed over how Athens can bring its debts down to a sustainable level.\par \par \uc2 The public disagreement highlights risks that Greece may not receive financial aid in time to avert bankruptcy, causing investors to duck for cover in safe-haven assets such as the dollar.\par \par \uc2 MEXICAN PESO TRIMS LOSSES\par \par \uc2 Sentiment improved slightly in the afternoon, cushioning the Mexican peso's losses, as key U.S. retail stocks were supported by strong results from \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 , the world's largest home improvement company.\par \par \uc2 The peso edged 0.1 percent lower at 13.2225 per dollar after dropping more than half a percentage point earlier. Mexico greatly relies on the U.S. consumer market to support its exports.\par \par \uc2 In a sign that \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  will maintain its non-\b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Deputy Finance Minister Gerardo Rodriguez said the government was not worried about the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness.\par \par \uc2 The recent decline in the peso's value, he said, reflects investor worries about the global economic outlook, but does not mean the danger of capital exodus has increased in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 2040 GMT\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                            Daily  Year-to\par                                      pct     date\par                          Latest   change      pct\par                                            change\par Brazil real                2.0575    -0.36    -9.19\par                                           \par Mexico peso               13.2225    -0.08     5.65\par                                           \par Argentina peso*            6.3000     0.32   -24.92\par                                           \par Chile peso               483.6000    -0.68     7.38\par                                           \par Colombia peso          1,816.8000    -0.11     6.69\par                                           \par Peru sol                   2.6050     0.27     3.53\par                                           \par * Argentine peso's rate between                    \par brokerages                                  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Writing by Walter Brandimarte; editing by Jan Paschal and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Leslie Adler\f28 \uc2 \f2 )\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | U | C | O | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121116e8bd00108\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
