\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-ANALYSIS-Peru  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  set to gain in 2013, defying  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Teresa Cespedes  \par \uc2 1,479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2012\par 21:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to widen distribution)\par \par \uc2 LIMA, Oct 17 (Reuters) - Big mining investments and extra U.S. monetary stimulus are set to extend a rise in Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has already taken the sol to a 16-year high despite sweeping official efforts.\par \par \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has bought a record $11.6 billion this year in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the spot market, faces a dilemma because it has a limited number of tools to avert sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings in the partially dollarized economy, and the finance ministry has ruled out using capital controls that would limit the sol's advances.\par \par \uc2 That basically leaves the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  the option of buying even more dollars on the spot market, allowing pension funds to put more money overseas to boost demand for dollars, or raising reserve requirements for bank deposits or loans.\par \par \uc2 These tools have been employed in varying doses over the last few years. But they have only slowed the pace of appreciation, not stopped it, and the chief of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says the sol might retreat only several years from now.\par \par \uc2 Though many Latin American countries face appreciation pressures that hurt export competitiveness, \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a more difficult job than most of its peers.\par \par \uc2 That's because more than 40 percent of all bank deposits in Peru are denominated in dollars and loans denominated in dollars are widely available. Right now, with the sol gaining, some Peruvian consumers are rushing into loans in dollars - betting their monthly payments will fall over time in domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  terms.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it is worried they may eventually get stung and that it must limit volatility to prevent consumers who get paid in soles but owe debts in dollars from being squeezed. This concern among policymakers outweighs complaints from exporters that a stronger sol will dent the country's economic growth streak of 36 straight months.\par \par \uc2 "Our primary worry is vulnerability from loans in dollars. We don't want the sol to appreciate beyond what fundamentals merit because at some point it will retreat," \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Julio Velarde told Congress on Wednesday. "This could happen in three, four or five years. We are concerned about what could happen with the financial system when it does retreat ... so it's better to try to prevent (excessive strengthening)."\par \par \uc2 In addition to the expected impact from more stimulus announced by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  in September, Peru's large fiscal surplus, low inflation and surging domestic demand have attracted new investors. They have been encouraged by surging domestic demand that has offset a dip in the country's vast mineral exports over the past few months.\par \par \uc2 "There is a restructuring of portfolios going on in favor of the sol. There is a perception among people in the market that the sol is a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and therefore they're betting on it and holding assets in soles," said Jorge Chavez, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president and chief of the Maximixe brokerage in Lima.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll on Oct. 4 of 17 emerging market economists showed the sol holding almost steady at 2.57 for the next 12 months.. But the feeling among nearly 10 Peru specialists consulted by Reuters is considerably more bullish, with their median forecast for late 2013 at 2.45 for a gain of 5.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 Ricardo V. Lago, a former \uc2 World Bank\uc2  official who has long been a fan of Peru's economy, forecast the most gains for the sol - 7 percent to 2.40 per dollar by the end of 2013. It is currently at a 16-year-high of about 2.578 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 It has strengthened 4.4 percent so far this year and is expected to gain another 1.2 percent to 2.55 per dollar by the end of 2012, the local economists said.\par \par \uc2 The sol has appreciated despite heavy and frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Those purchases, which Velarde has called the biggest in the region for the size of Peru's economy, have averted gains that might have been larger.\par \par \uc2 In neighboring Chile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year and in \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  it has largely held back from doing so. The peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of both those countries have appreciated about twice as much as the sol in 2012.\par \par \uc2 In the last decade, the sol has gained 26 percent, helping a "de-dollarization" of the economy. Ten years ago, 7 0 p ercent of Peruvian bank deposits were held in dollar-denominated accounts. Now only 43 percent are.\par \par \uc2 "I would say the sol will be at 2.40 per dollar at the close of 2013," Lago said. "The sol will continue to appreciate, and even if there were a bump in the road in 2013 because of the European crisis we can expect continued economic growth in 2015 and 2016. Plus, the avalanche of mining projects supports this hypothesis."\par \par \uc2 Peru has a portfolio of mining projects worth $53 billion in coming years, though some of them have been held up by social protests. Foreign direct investment and portfolio inflows over the last 3.5 years were $30 billion, more than neighboring Bolivia's gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 APPETITE FOR SOLES\par \par \uc2 Velarde said he has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently in the local spot market because the "appetite for the sol is amazing" and that, unlike years past, it has become a darling of investors.\par \par \uc2 "The situation has reversed and now it's the world's most conventional funds, the most conservative ones, that are buying Peru," Velarde said in late September.\par \par \uc2 Powered by domestic demand, Peru's economy is expected to grow 6 percent this year, the fastest pace in South America, and expand at a similar annual rate through 2014.\par \par \uc2 International reserves have swollen to $61 billion - equal to a third of GDP. Inflation is mild, running about 3 percent a year, while the fiscal surplus is healthy. It widened to 7 percent of GDP in the first half of this year, and is seen at 1.5 percent of GDP for the year and 1 percent in 2013.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, investment inflows more than cover the current account gap - forecast at 3.9 percent of GDP this year because of a downturn in mineral exports.\par \par \uc2 "These are the elements that will determine the exchange rate in the medium term. They suggest the exchange rate will continue to fall," said Hugo Perea, the head of economic research at \uc2 BBVA\uc2  Banco Continental.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  MEASURES\par \par \uc2 The sol's appreciation trend is expected to intensify with spillover from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's decision to buy $40 billion in mortgage securities a month until the labor market improves.\par \par \uc2 "When these monetary packages are announced, we all know that these flows will end up in the whole world and thus won't be reflected in accelerated inflation in the U.S.," said Chavez, the former Peruvian central banker. "This would tend to intensify the sol's gains."\par \par \uc2 Economists expect \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to announce more measures to keep excessive capital inflows from creating systemic risks or credit bubbles.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already raised bank deposit requirements three times this year to stem the impact of foreign capital inflows and reduce the pace of credit growth.\par \par \uc2 Velarde has said two additional measures are being evaluated, including asking banks to set aside more reserves for home mortgages denominated in dollars, an idea which is being reviewed by the country's banking regulator.\par \par \uc2 New mortgages in dollars, which carry lower interest rates than loans in soles, lea p t 23.8 percent in July on the year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said consumers should not aggressively sign up for new dollar loans by betting their monthly payments will fall in the future as the sol gains. Consumers with mortgages in many [20121017 192631 GMT]\par \par \uc2 Eastern European countries suffered in recent years when local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciated.\par \par \uc2 Another measure, which has been used in the past, would boost demand for dollars by allowing local pension funds to invest more abroad. Current rules limit them to putting 30 percent of their $30 billion in funds overseas. The ceiling might be slowly lifted to 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 "This is common sense and should be done because it wouldn't require much effort on the part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it wouldn't imply inappropriate risks," said Chavez.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Omar Mariluz; Editing by Terry Wade and W Simon) [20121017 192631 GMT]\par \par \uc2 PERU-ECONOMY/CENTRALBANK (ANALYSIS)(REPEAT) | ABN | M | NAT | D | ABX | BNX | FUN | C | GRO | MTL | SOF | E | RBN | O | U | OIL|PERU-ECONOMY/CENTRALBANK (ANALYSIS)(REPEAT)=2 | ABN | M | NAT | D | ABX | BNX | FUN | C | GRO | MTL | SOF | E | RBN | O | U | OIL|chained  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121020e8ah0009t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
